:: Doctor Harper Autism Check-up
<<set $outside to 0>><<set $location to "hospital">><<effects>>
<<harper_intro>>
Harper takes a blank form from the drawer and turns <<his>> chair towards you.
"Tell me what made you ask about this."
<br><br>
You start with the sounds.
<br><br>
Some sounds make it hard to keep thinking. While they continue, you can lose track of what you were doing or saying.
When the noise stops, you need time to work out where the thought went before you can continue.
<br><br>
Harper writes as you speak. When you finish, <<he>> asks how long this has happened and whether you remember having the
same problem when you were younger.
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Doctor Harper Autism Check-up Questions]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Doctor Harper Autism Check-up Questions
<<effects>>
Harper moves on to other senses. <<He>> asks about bright light, unexpected touch, crowded rooms, and whether any of them
can make it difficult to think or speak.
<br><br>
Then <<he>> asks about conversations. What happens when somebody hints instead of saying what they want? Do you have to
work out when it is your turn to speak? Do people sometimes read the expression on your face differently from how you feel?
<br><br>
The last questions are about routine. Harper asks how you react when plans change without warning, what habits you prefer
to keep the same, and whether there are subjects or activities you can stay with for hours.
<br><br>
When <<he>> reaches the bottom of the form, <<he>> reads over the notes before continuing.
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Doctor Harper Autism Check-up 1]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Doctor Harper Autism Check-up 1
<<effects>>
Harper reads over what <<he>> has written, then sets the pen beside the form.
<br><br>
"I want to be careful with this," <<he>> says. "What you have described can fit autism, but some of it can also happen
for other reasons. I do not want you leaving this room with a diagnosis after one short appointment."
<br><br>
"ADHD is one of the things I need to consider. Autism and ADHD can happen in the same person, and some difficulties can
look similar from the outside. A proper assessment gives us more information before I decide what belongs where."
<br><br>
"I am going to refer you for that assessment," <<he>> says. "It takes longer than this appointment, because we look at
what you are like now and what you were like when you were younger."
<br><br>
<<link [[Ask what the assessment involves|Doctor Harper Autism Check-up 2]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Doctor Harper Autism Check-up 2
<<effects>>
<<npc Harper>><<person1>>
"The assessment is usually divided into three parts," Harper says.
<br><br>
"First, I take a developmental history. I ask about your childhood as well as how things are for you now."
<br><br>
"Second, we collect other information where we can. That can include school records, a teacher, somebody responsible for
you at home, and questionnaires you fill in yourself."
<br><br>
"Third, you come back here. I compare everything we have and explain the result to you."
<br><br>
<<He>> looks at you before continuing. "You do not need a parent to do this. Information from somebody who knew you when
you were younger can help, but the assessment does not stop because you cannot provide it."
<br><br>
<<link [[Ask about the waiting time and cost|Doctor Harper Autism Check-up 3]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Doctor Harper Autism Check-up 3
<<effects>>
Harper pulls the referral form closer. "There are two routes. The first one costs you nothing, but the waiting list is long.
You could be waiting several months before the first assessment appointment."
<br><br>
"The other route is private. They can see you this week and should finish the assessment within two weeks.
It costs <<printprice `autismPrivateCost()`>>."
<br><br>
<<He>> sets the form down. "Money changes how quickly people get assessed. If your family could have paid for this earlier,
you might have been assessed years ago. I do not like that part of the system, but I want you to know why the two waits are
so different."
<br><br>
<<link [[Go on the waiting list|Doctor Harper Autism Referral]]>><<run autismSetAssessmentRoute("nhs")>><<run autismAssessmentSetStage("raised")>><</link>>
<br>
<<if autismCanAffordPrivate()>>
	<<link [[Pay for a private assessment (<<printprice `autismPrivateCost()`>>)|Doctor Harper Autism Referral]]>><<money `-autismPrivateCost()` "autismAssessment">><<run autismSetAssessmentRoute("private")>><<run autismAssessmentSetStage("raised")>><</link>>
	<br>
<<else>>
	<<ind>><span class="blue">You cannot afford a private assessment (<<printprice `autismPrivateCost()`>>).</span>
	<br>
<</if>>
<<link [[Say you would rather not|Doctor Harper Autism Referral Decline]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Doctor Harper Autism Referral
<<set $outside to 0>><<set $location to "hospital">><<effects>>
<<set $autismSpectrum.harperDiscussed to true>>
Harper fills in the referral while you sit beside the desk. <<He>> reads each field aloud before entering it, so you can
hear exactly what <<hes>> putting on the form.
<br><br>
<<if autismAssessmentRoute() is "private">>
	<<He>> checks the appointment screen and frowns. "They will see you this week."
	<br><br>
	"Come back here in a few days and we will do the developmental history. It takes about an hour. If school or work has
	already exhausted you that day, choose another day if you can."
	<br><br>
	<<He>> writes the appointment on a card and hands it to you. "This only has the appointment and my name on it.
	There is no report yet."
	<br><br>
	You have paid for a private assessment. <span class="green">Return to Harper in a few days</span>.
<<else>>
	<<He>> checks the referral screen. "You are on the waiting list."
	<br><br>
	"It will take a while. I would rather tell you that clearly than have you wonder whether the referral disappeared.
	You are still on the list; there are many people waiting ahead of you."
	<br><br>
	<<He>> writes a return date and <<his>> name on a card, then hands it to you. "Keep this somewhere you will find it later."
	<br><br>
	You are on the waiting list. It will be <span class="green">some weeks before the first appointment</span>.
	<br>
	You can <span class="gold">pay to go private later</span> if you would rather not wait.
<</if>>
<br><br>
<<link [[Return to the foyer|Hospital Foyer]]>><<endevent>><</link>>
<br>

:: Doctor Harper Autism Go Private
<<set $outside to 0>><<set $location to "hospital">><<effects>>
<<harper_intro>>
You tell Harper that you want to stop waiting and use the private route.
<br><br>
<<money `-autismPrivateCost()` "autismAssessment">><<run autismSetAssessmentRoute("private")>>
<<He>> opens the referral again and fills in a second form without asking where the money came from.
<br><br>
"Paying gets you seen faster," <<he>> says. "I dislike that part of the system, but it will move your appointment to this week."
<br><br>
<<He>> checks the new date before looking back at you. "I will see you within the week."
<br><br>
You have paid for a private assessment. <span class="green">The wait is over</span>.
<br><br>
<<link [[Return to the foyer|Hospital Foyer]]>><<endevent>><</link>>
<br>

:: Doctor Harper Autism Referral Decline
<<set $outside to 0>><<set $location to "hospital">><<effects>>
<<set $autismSpectrum.harperDiscussed to true>>
You say that you would rather think about it first.
<br><br>
Harper closes the form. "That is fine. I will not send a referral until you ask me to."
<br><br>
"If you change your mind later, come back and tell me. We can start from here without repeating this appointment."
<br><br>
<<link [[Return to the foyer|Hospital Foyer]]>><<endevent>><</link>>
<br>

:: Doctor Harper Autism Intake
<<set $outside to 0>><<set $location to "hospital">><<effects>>
<<harper_intro>>
<<run autismAssessmentSetStage("intake")>>
The appointment lasts close to an hour. Harper asks about you for most of it, so even simple questions start to become tiring.
<br><br>
<<He>> begins with how things are now, then moves backwards through last year, when you were eleven, and as early as you can
remember. When you cannot remember something, <<he>> writes that down and moves to the next question.
<br><br>
"Did you line things up when you were younger? Did you start talking later than other children, or talk in a way people
thought was unusual? Were there clothes or fabrics you refused to wear?"
<br><br>
Some examples fit you and others do not. Harper records both kinds instead of trying to make every question fit.
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Doctor Harper Autism Intake 1]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Doctor Harper Autism Intake 1
<<effects>>
A few questions are about attention rather than autism. Harper writes those answers in a separate column so <<he>> can
compare them later without mixing the two together.
<br><br>
When <<he>> finishes the questions, <<he>> reaches for a folder.
<br><br>
"I also need information from outside this room where we can get it," <<he>> says. "That gives me something to compare
with what you have told me today."
<br><br>
<<He>> puts the folder in front of you. "Some of this is for you. The other forms can go to your school or to somebody
who knows what you were like when you were younger."
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Doctor Harper Autism Intake 2]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Doctor Harper Autism Intake 2
<<effects>>
"There are questionnaires in the folder for you," Harper says. "There is also a form for your school and one for somebody
responsible for you at home."
<br><br>
"Bring back as many completed forms as you can. One gives me another source to compare with your answers. Several give me
more points of comparison. If nobody fills one in, we continue with the information we do have."
<br><br>
<<He>> closes the folder and pushes it across the desk. "Come back after the paperwork has had time to arrive. Give it about
a week, then I can go through the result with you."
<br><br>
You have the assessment folder. Gather what you can, then <span class="green">return to Harper</span>.
<br><br>
<<link [[Return to the foyer|Hospital Foyer]]>><<endevent>><</link>>
<br>

:: Doctor Harper Autism Feedback
<<set $outside to 0>><<set $location to "hospital">><<effects>>
<<harper_intro>>
<<set _sources to autismAssessmentGathered()>>
Harper already has your file open when you sit down. Several forms have been added since your last appointment.
<br><br>
<<if _sources.length gte 3>>
	"All of the forms came back," <<he>> says. "That gives me several independent accounts to compare with what you told me."
<<elseif _sources.length gte 2>>
	"Most of the forms came back," <<he>> says. "The useful part is that the different people are describing the same kinds of difficulties."
<<else>>
	"Fewer forms came back than I wanted," <<he>> says. "I can still make an assessment, but I have to rely more heavily
	on what you told me yourself."
<</if>>
<br><br>
<<He>> turns the file so you can see it and points to several marked lines.
<br><br>
"I keep finding the same areas from childhood through to now: communication, routines and changes, your reactions to sound,
light and touch, and the amount of attention you can give to particular interests."
<br><br>
<<He>> taps the page once. "Taken together, this meets the criteria for Autism Spectrum. That is the result I am putting in
the report."
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Doctor Harper Autism Feedback 1]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Doctor Harper Autism Feedback 1
<<effects>>
Harper turns to another page. "Autism can affect people in very different ways. The report describes the areas that affect
you instead of trying to place you on one simple scale."
<br><br>
"Some autistic people repeat words. Some needed more time to develop speech or movement when they were young. Some have
strong reactions to sound, touch or changes in routine. Other people have a different combination."
<br><br>
"An intense interest can be part of it as well. That does not mean every autistic person has a special talent, and it does
not mean every strong interest is the same."
<br><br>
<<He>> turns the page back towards <<him>>. "Your report is about the pattern we found in you."
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Doctor Harper Autism Feedback 2]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Doctor Harper Autism Feedback 2
<<effects>>
"I looked at attention as well," Harper says, tapping a second column. "I saw signs that may be worth checking again,
but I do not have the evidence I would need to diagnose a second condition today."
<br><br>
"If attention starts causing more problems, tell me and we can assess it separately. I would rather leave that question
open than write down something I am not sure about."
<br><br>
<<He>> points to the section about accumulated demand. "For now, shorten your work sessions when you are already worn out.
Leave time between difficult tasks. Eat, wash and sleep before trying to force another long session into the same day."
<br><br>
"The practical goal is to make daily life easier and reduce the amount of overload you have to push through. Support should
help you do that. You do not need to spend more effort hiding autistic behaviour just to make other people comfortable."
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Doctor Harper Autism Feedback 3]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Doctor Harper Autism Feedback 3
<<effects>>
Harper signs the last page, then clips a shorter sheet to the front.
<br><br>
"The long one is the full report. The short one is a summary. It lists the adjustments I recommend for school, so you can
show that page without handing over the whole assessment."
<br><br>
<<run autismAssessmentSetStage("diagnosed")>>
You have <span class="green">diagnostic documentation</span> and a summary of recommended adjustments.
Harper slides both sheets towards you and waits while you look over the first page. <<He>> speaks when you look back up.
<br><br>
"Tell me what is actually going through your head. You do not need to react in any particular way."
<br><br>
<<link [[Say it is a relief to have a name for it|Doctor Harper Autism Now Relief]]>><</link>>
<br>
<<link [[Say you do not feel much of anything|Doctor Harper Autism Now Flat]]>><</link>>
<br>
<<link [[Say you are worried about who will use this against you|Doctor Harper Autism Now Worried]]>><</link>>
<br>
<<link [[Ask what it actually does for you day to day|Doctor Harper Autism Now Practical]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Doctor Harper Autism Now Relief
<<effects>>
You say that the result is a relief. Problems that used to feel separate now have one explanation you can point to,
and that makes them easier to understand.
<br><br>
Harper nods. "A lot of people tell me that."
<br><br>
"You were dealing with the same things last week. Today gives you a name for them and a report you can use when you need
to explain those needs to somebody else."
<<stress -8>><<lstress>><<trauma -6>><<ltrauma>>
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Autism Identity]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Doctor Harper Autism Now Flat
<<effects>>
You say that you do not feel much of anything. You expected a stronger reaction after waiting this long, but the result has landed without much feeling attached to it.
<br><br>
Harper puts <<his>> pen down instead of immediately writing that answer into the report.
<br><br>
"I'm glad you said that plainly," <<he>> says. "People sometimes think they are supposed to feel relieved, upset, or suddenly certain about themselves when I give them a diagnosis. I would rather hear what is actually happening for you."
<br><br>
<<He>> turns the report back towards <<him>>. "You may feel differently after you have had a few days to think about it. You may feel differently months from now, or you may never have a strong reaction at all. None of those possibilities changes what we found during the assessment."
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Autism Identity]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Doctor Harper Autism Now Worried
<<effects>>
You tell Harper what is worrying you: there is now a medical record describing things other people already call difficult,
and you can think of people who might use it against you.
<br><br>
You expected <<him>> to dismiss the concern, but <<he>> keeps listening until you finish.
<br><br>
"That is a reasonable fear," <<he>> says. "A report can be misused, so you should know who receives it."
<br><br>
"This stays in your medical record. I do not send a copy to your school, your guardian or anybody else unless you choose
to share it with them."
<br><br>
<<if C.npc.Bailey.love lt 20>>
	<<He>> pauses before adding, "That includes the person at home you were thinking about."
<</if>>
<<stress -4>><<lstress>>
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Autism Identity]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Doctor Harper Autism Now Practical
<<effects>>
You ask what the report can actually change in your day-to-day life.
<br><br>
"The short sheet lists adjustments the school can use," Harper says. "For example: instructions given one at a time,
written instructions as well as spoken ones, and somewhere quiet you can go when a room becomes too difficult to stay in."
<br><br>
"It can also record that leaving for that quiet space is part of the adjustment, so staff do not automatically treat it
as walking out of a lesson."
<br><br>
"The school has to consider the recommendations and record what it decides. That does not guarantee every teacher will
handle them well."
<br><br>
"Sometimes one teacher who understands what you need will make a larger difference than the paperwork on its own.
You know your teachers better than I do."
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Autism Identity]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Doctor Harper Autism Now End
<<set $outside to 0>><<set $location to "hospital">><<effects>>
"One more thing, then I will let you go."
<br><br>
Harper rests one hand on the folder. "You decide who sees this. The hospital keeps the report in your medical record,
but you choose whether you show the summary or report to somebody outside here."
<br><br>
"Some people show the school straight away. Some wait until they need a specific adjustment. Some only show one person
they trust." <<He>> shrugs. "You can decide separately each time."
<br><br>
<<if $harper_appointments.enabled>>
	<<He>> closes the folder. "You already see me on Fridays. Write down questions when they come up and bring them with you."
	<br><br>
	<<link [[Return to the foyer|Hospital Foyer]]>><<endevent>><</link>>
<<else>>
	Harper leaves the appointment page open on <<his>> computer. "I can see you here once a week on Fridays if you want
	somewhere regular to bring questions. Shall I put you down?"
	<br><br>
	<<link [[Agree to the weekly check-up|Doctor Harper Autism Appointment]]>><<set $harper_appointments.enabled to true>><<unset $schoolPsych>><</link>>
	<br>
	<<link [[Decline|Doctor Harper Autism Decline]]>><</link>>
<</if>>
<br>

:: Autism Rent Explain
<<if autismRentCanPay()>>
	You explain that the money was ready, but every time you meant to bring it to Bailey, you put off starting the conversation and never handed it over.
	<br><br>
	Bailey lets you finish, then holds out <<his>> hand. "So the money was sitting there the whole time while you kept thinking about giving it to me?"
	<br><br>
	You nod. <<He>> gives a short laugh. "That's a new excuse. Creative, even. Still doesn't change what you owe."
<<else>>
	You explain that you remembered the rent every day, but remembering it never led to you starting what you needed to do.
	<br><br>
	Bailey folds <<his>> arms. "So you spent the whole week thinking about paying me and still didn't get as far as the money. Brilliant."
	<br><br>
	<<He>> holds out <<his>> hand. "Thinking about rent doesn't pay rent. Put whatever you've got here."
<</if>>
<<stress 4>><<gstress>>
<<if autismDisclosedTo("Bailey")>>
	<br>
	<<He>> does not mention the report. The folder is still on the shelf behind <<him>>, but <<he>> does not reduce what you owe.
<<elseif autismHasDocumentation()>>
	<br>
	You do not mention the report. Bailey has already made it clear that <<hes>> only interested in the missing payment.
<</if>>

:: Autism Disclose Bailey
<<set $outside to 0>><<set $location to "home">><<effects>>
<<npc Bailey>><<person1>>
You take out the summary sheet before you change your mind and hold it across the desk.
<br><br>
Bailey takes it, scans the hospital letterhead, then reads the first few lines. <<His>> eyes move back to you before <<he>> reaches the bottom.
<br><br>
"Alright," <<he>> says, keeping one finger on the page. "You've brought me a doctor's report. What am I supposed to do with it?"
<br><br>
<<link [[Say you wanted <<him>> to know|Autism Disclose Bailey Know]]>><</link>>
<br>
<<link [[Say it explains some of how you are|Autism Disclose Bailey Explain]]>><</link>>
<br>
<<link [[Ask whether it changes anything|Autism Disclose Bailey Owe]]>><</link>>
<br>
<<link [[Take it back. This was a mistake|Autism Disclose Bailey Regret]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Disclose Bailey Know
<<effects>>
You say you only wanted <<him>> to know. You are not asking <<him>> to change anything.
<br><br>
Bailey leans back in <<his>> chair and studies you for several seconds, then looks down at the sheet again.
<br><br>
<<if C.npc.Bailey.love gte 40>>
	"Alright. You wanted me to know, so now I know," <<he>> says. <<He>> taps one paragraph with <<his>> thumb. "The noise thing makes sense. You used to cover your ears in the hall when you were little, especially when everyone was shouting over each other."
	<br><br>
	You do not remember doing that. Bailey does, and <<he>> describes it without needing to think.
	<br><br>
	<<He>> hands the sheet back. "Doesn't change Sunday, mind. I'm still expecting the money."
<<else>>
	"Fine. You've told me," <<he>> says, passing the sheet back across the desk. "I'm not throwing you a party for it, but I'm not going to pretend you never said anything either."
	<br><br>
	<<He>> waits another second in case you are about to ask for something. When you stay quiet, <<he>> pulls the ledger back towards <<him>>.
<</if>>
<br><br>
<<run autismDiscloseTo("Bailey")>>
<<link [[Next|Orphanage]]>><<endevent>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Disclose Bailey Explain
<<effects>>
You explain that when you go quiet, you are sometimes struggling to speak rather than deliberately ignoring <<him>>. Noise, sudden changes, and several demands arriving together can make it harder to work out what to do next.
<br><br>
Bailey keeps one hand on the ledger but lets you finish before <<he>> answers.
<br><br>
<<if C.npc.Bailey.love gte 40>>
	"Some of that sounds like you," <<he>> says. "Some of it sounds like being fourteen and pissed off at the world, which I also know plenty about from this place."
	<br><br>
	<<He>> pushes the report back towards you. "I'm not going to sit here deciding which bad day belongs in which box. I haven't got the time, and I'd probably get it wrong. But if you tell me you can't answer straight away, I'll remember you said that."
<<else>>
	"So a doctor has finally put in writing that you're difficult," <<he>> says. "I've been telling people that for years and nobody ever paid me for the diagnosis."
	<br><br>
	<<He>> gives the page another look before sliding it back. "If there's something on there I legally have to do, tell me. Otherwise I've still got a house full of kids and a ledger that doesn't care why anyone had a bad morning."<<stress 6>><<gstress>>
<</if>>
<br><br>
<<run autismDiscloseTo("Bailey")>>
<<link [[Next|Orphanage]]>><<endevent>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Disclose Bailey Owe
<<effects>>
You ask whether the diagnosis changes what Bailey expects from you.
<br><br>
Bailey stops writing, places the pen across the ledger, and looks directly at you.
<br><br>
"There it is. You want to know whether that piece of paper changes what you owe me," <<he>> says. "No. Rent was rent yesterday and it's rent today. I'm not saying the report means nothing; I'm saying it doesn't pay the bills."
<br><br>
<<He>> lifts the sheet between two fingers. "Does the hospital give you money? Does school start paying me because you've got this? Does anyone cover your Sunday payment?"
<br><br>
You tell <<him>> no.
<br><br>
"Then keep the report for the things it actually helps with," <<he>> says, handing it back. "And keep bringing me the money."
<br><br>
<<run autismDiscloseTo("Bailey")>>
<<link [[Next|Orphanage]]>><<endevent>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Disclose Bailey Regret
<<effects>>
You reach across the desk and take the sheet back before Bailey can finish reading it.
<br><br>
<<He>> lets go without trying to stop you. "If you don't want me reading it, don't wave it under my nose next time," <<he>> says, already turning back to the ledger.
<br><br>
You put the report away and head towards the stairs. Bailey's reaction follows you: you had shown <<him>> something personal, and <<he>> had immediately started looking for what it changed in the running of the orphanage.<<stress 4>><<gstress>>
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Orphanage]]>><<endevent>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Disclose Leighton
<<set $outside to 0>><<set $location to "school">><<schooleffects>><<effects>>
<<npc Leighton>><<person1>>
<<set _suspicious to $delinquency gte setup.schoolDiscipline.dutyThreshold>>
You place the summary sheet on Leighton's desk and explain that it came from the hospital.
<br><br>
<<He>> reads the page from top to bottom, checks the back, then returns to the section listing recommended adjustments.
<br><br>
<<if _suspicious>>
	<<He>> opens your school record beside it and compares the two documents.
	<br><br>
	"Interesting timing," <<he>> says. "A medical report in one hand and a disciplinary record in the other. I assume you understand why I am going to read both before I decide what changes."
<<else>>
	<<He>> taps the hospital heading with one fingernail. "These are recommendations from a clinician, not a command for the school to abandon its rules. That is workable."
<</if>>
<br><br>
<<link [[Say you are asking for adjustments, not exemption|Autism Disclose Leighton Adjust]]>><</link>>
<br>
<<link [[Say nothing and let <<him>> finish reading|Autism Disclose Leighton Quiet]]>><</link>>
<br>
<<link [[Point to the two things you actually need|Autism Disclose Leighton Specific]]>><</link>>
<br>
<<link [[Take it back before it goes on any record|Autism Disclose Leighton Withdraw]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Disclose Leighton Adjust
<<effects>>
<<set _suspicious to $delinquency gte setup.schoolDiscipline.dutyThreshold>>
You tell Leighton that you are not asking to be excused from lessons or discipline. You want the school to use the adjustments listed on the report.
<br><br>
<<if _suspicious>>
	"That is a much better place to begin," Leighton says. "I was about to ask whether you expected this page to erase the other one."
	<br><br>
	<<He>> turns the screen so your disciplinary record is visible beside the report. "There is quite a lot recorded here. Some incidents may make more sense with this information, but an explanation does not turn every consequence into a mistake. I am not reopening the entire file."
<<else>>
	Leighton's eyebrows lift before <<he>> returns to the report.
	<br><br>
	"Most students who bring me paperwork begin with what they believe they can stop doing," <<he>> says. "You have saved us both a tedious conversation by starting with what you actually need."
<</if>>
<br><br>
<<He>> places the report beside the keyboard. "I will record the diagnosis and circulate the listed adjustments to your teachers this week. That means staff have been told what to change. It does not mean you can leave a lesson without telling anyone where you are going, and it does not remove the rest of the school rules."
<br><br>
<<run autismSetSchoolPlan()>><<run autismDiscloseTo("Leighton")>><<run autismSetDisclosureDepth("Leighton", "brief")>>
<<link [[Next|Hallways]]>><<endevent>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Disclose Leighton Quiet
<<effects>>
<<set _suspicious to $delinquency gte setup.schoolDiscipline.dutyThreshold>>
You stay quiet while Leighton finishes reading.
<br><br>
When <<he>> reaches the bottom of the page, <<he>> waits for you to explain why you brought it. You still say nothing.
<br><br>
<<if _suspicious>>
	"No defence, no speech about how unfair the school is, and no attempt to bargain down your record," <<he>> says. "Given your history, that is almost refreshing."
	<br><br>
	<<He>> sets the report beside your disciplinary file. "Since you have brought me a medical document rather than an argument, I will treat it as one."
<<else>>
	Leighton sets the page down. "You are apparently content to let the document speak for you. I can work with that."
<</if>>
<br><br>
"I will put it on your record and send the adjustments to your teachers. That is procedure; it does not require either of us to enjoy this conversation."
<br><br>
<<He>> reaches for the next file on <<his>> desk. "Use the adjustments properly and do not make me regret being reasonable."
<br><br>
<<run autismSetSchoolPlan()>><<run autismDiscloseTo("Leighton")>><<run autismSetDisclosureDepth("Leighton", "brief")>>
<<link [[Next|Hallways]]>><<endevent>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Disclose Leighton Specific
<<effects>>
You lean over the desk and point to two recommendations on the sheet: permission to leave for the infirmary when the room becomes too difficult, and instructions given in smaller steps with the important parts written down.
<br><br>
"Those are the two I actually need," you say. "The rest can stay on the file."
<br><br>
Leighton reads both lines again.
<br><br>
"The instruction change is simple," <<he>> says. "It costs the school nothing, and several teachers should already be doing it."
<br><br>
<<He>> taps the line about leaving class. "This one needs a route we can record. If you have to leave, you go to the infirmary. You do not go to the gates, the field, or the car park. The nurse records that you arrived. If it starts happening often, I will want to know why and whether the arrangement still works."
<br><br>
<<if $delinquency gte setup.schoolDiscipline.dutyThreshold>>
	<<He>> glances at your existing record. "And before you ask later, this applies from today. It does not erase what is already written there."
<</if>>
<br><br>
<<run autismSetSchoolPlan()>><<run autismDiscloseTo("Leighton")>><<run autismSetDisclosureDepth("Leighton", "full")>>
<<link [[Next|Hallways]]>><<endevent>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Disclose Leighton Withdraw
<<effects>>
You reach across and take the sheet back before <<he>> has finished with it.
<br><br>
Leighton lets go of the sheet and rests <<his>> hand back on the desk.
<br><br>
"It is not recorded," <<he>> says. "You brought a piece of paper into my office and left with it. That is all
that happened, and I have a great deal to do."
<br><br>
<<He>> returns to <<his>> screen. Then, without looking up: "It stays available. I do not chase these and I will
not raise it with you. If you want it on the file, bring it back."
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Hallways]]>><<endevent>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Disclose Nurse
<<set $outside to 0>><<set $location to "school">><<schooleffects>><<effects>>
<<if $per_npc.school_nurse>><<loadNPC 0 school_nurse>><<else>><<generate1>><<saveNPC 0 school_nurse>><</if>><<person1>>
You show the nurse the summary sheet.
<br><br>
<<He>> reads it quickly, then looks at you. "What actually helps when this happens?"
<br><br>
"I cannot change your timetable," <<he>> says before you answer. "The office handles that. What I can do is let you use this room, and I can write down what staff should do if you become overwhelmed."
<br><br>
<<He>> takes a card from the file and uncaps a pen.
<br><br>
"Two questions. When it is getting bad, what happens first? And when it has got bad, what do you want someone
to do?"
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Autism Disclose Nurse 2]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Disclose Nurse 2
<<effects>>
You answer the first question as honestly as you can.
<br><br>
<<if autismLastOverloadForm() is "shutdown" and autismOverloadDaysAgo() gte 0>>
	You tell <<him>> about the last shutdown. You could hear people speaking, but you could not organise what you heard into an answer. People kept repeating the
	question, sometimes louder, while you were still trying to form a reply.
	<br><br>
	<<He>> writes <i>may lose speech; can still hear you</i> and underlines the second half twice.
<<else>>
	You explain that noise is usually the first sign. When too many sounds and demands arrive together, you struggle
	to separate them. Speaking becomes harder after that because you have to work through everything else first.
	<br><br>
	<<He>> writes your answer down almost word for word.
<</if>>
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Autism Disclose Nurse 3]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Disclose Nurse 3
<<effects>>
The nurse turns the card over. "Second question. If things have already become difficult, what do you want staff to do?"
<br><br>
<<He>> waits with the pen above the card. "Tell me what actually helps. You do not need to choose what sounds easiest
for the staff."
<br><br>
You can choose <span class="gold">up to two things</span> to put on the card.
<br><br>
<<link [[Give me space|Autism Nurse Pref]]>><<set $autismPrefChoice to "space">><</link>>
<br>
<<link [[Speak plainly, one instruction at a time|Autism Nurse Pref]]>><<set $autismPrefChoice to "plain">><</link>>
<br>
<<link [[Do not touch me without warning|Autism Nurse Pref]]>><<set $autismPrefChoice to "notouch">><</link>>
<br>
<<link [[Let me sit somewhere quiet|Autism Nurse Pref]]>><<set $autismPrefChoice to "quiet">><</link>>
<br>
<<link [[Fetch someone I trust, if you can|Autism Nurse Pref]]>><<set $autismPrefChoice to "person">><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Nurse Pref
<<effects>>
<<run autismAddPreference($autismPrefChoice)>>
<<switch $autismPrefChoice>>
	<<case "space">>You say you need people to move back. Not to leave, necessarily. Just to stop being close.
	<<case "plain">>You say you want one clear instruction at a time, followed by enough time to answer.
	<<case "notouch">>You say that being touched without warning makes it worse, and that people do it constantly, meaning well.
	<<case "quiet">>You say you need somewhere quiet with fewer people and less noise. Ten minutes is usually enough.
	<<default>>You say there are one or two people whose presence helps, and name them.
<</switch>>
<br><br>
<<He>> writes it on the card without interrupting or questioning your choice.
<br><br>
<<if autismPreferenceCount() lt 2>>
	"One more, if you have one."
	<br><br>
	<<if !autismHasPreference("space")>><<link [[Give me space|Autism Nurse Pref]]>><<set $autismPrefChoice to "space">><</link>><br><</if>>
	<<if !autismHasPreference("plain")>><<link [[Speak plainly, one instruction at a time|Autism Nurse Pref]]>><<set $autismPrefChoice to "plain">><</link>><br><</if>>
	<<if !autismHasPreference("notouch")>><<link [[Do not touch me without warning|Autism Nurse Pref]]>><<set $autismPrefChoice to "notouch">><</link>><br><</if>>
	<<if !autismHasPreference("quiet")>><<link [[Let me sit somewhere quiet|Autism Nurse Pref]]>><<set $autismPrefChoice to "quiet">><</link>><br><</if>>
	<<if !autismHasPreference("person")>><<link [[Fetch someone I trust, if you can|Autism Nurse Pref]]>><<set $autismPrefChoice to "person">><</link>><br><</if>>
	<<link [[That is enough|Autism Disclose Nurse End]]>><</link>>
	<br>
<<else>>
	<<link [[Next|Autism Disclose Nurse End]]>><</link>>
	<br>
<</if>>

:: Autism Disclose Nurse End
<<set $outside to 0>><<set $location to "school">><<schooleffects>><<effects>>
<<He>> puts the card in the file and closes it.
<br><br>
"Whoever is on duty will be able to read this. Staff may not follow it perfectly, but at least they will have clear instructions."
<br><br>
<<He>> nods towards the beds behind the curtain. "You can also come here before things get worse. You do not have to be ill or explain everything. Tell me you need the room and I will give you ten minutes. If a teacher asks why you left, I will say I called you here."
<br><br>
<<run autismDiscloseTo("nurse")>>
<<unset $autismPrefChoice>>
<<link [[Next|School Infirmary]]>><<endevent>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Disclose Teacher
<<set $outside to 0>><<set $location to "school">><<schooleffects>><<effects>>
<<set _teacher to $autismDiscloseTarget>>
<<switch _teacher>>
	<<case "Sirris">><<npc Sirris>>
	<<case "Doren">><<npc Doren>>
	<<case "Winter">><<npc Winter>>
	<<default>><<npc River>>
<</switch>><<person1>>
You wait until the room is mostly empty.
<br><br>
<<switch _teacher>>
	<<case "Doren">>Doren is stacking books on <<his>> desk when you approach.
	<<case "River">>River is already packing up and keeps doing so as you approach.
	<<case "Sirris">>Sirris looks up and smiles before you speak. You hesitate before holding out the sheet.
	<<default>>Winter is writing notes and does not look up until you are standing directly in front of the desk.
<</switch>>
<br><br>
<<link [[Just hand over the sheet and let it speak|Autism Disclose Teacher Brief]]>><</link>>
<br>
<<link [[Explain what it is actually like|Autism Disclose Teacher Full]]>><</link>>
<br>
<<link [[Change your mind and put it away|Autism Disclose Teacher Back]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Disclose Teacher Back
<<effects>>
You start to explain, then change your mind and put the sheet back in your bag.
<br><br>
<<if $autismDiscloseTarget is "Doren">>
	Doren stops stacking the books and gives you a small nod. "That's alright. If you decide you want to show me later, bring it back. You don't have to explain why you changed your mind."
<<elseif $autismDiscloseTarget is "River">>
	River watches the paper disappear into your bag. "Changed your mind? Fine. Tell me if there's something I need to know for the lesson." <<He>> goes back to packing.
<<elseif $autismDiscloseTarget is "Sirris">>
	Sirris starts to ask whether you are sure, sees you closing the bag, and catches <<himself>>. "Alright. I won't press you. You know where I am if you want to talk later."
<<else>>
	Winter watches you put the sheet away. "If you do not want to discuss it, then do not begin the conversation," <<he>> says, before returning to <<his>> notes. "If that changes, come prepared to tell me what you need."
<</if>>
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Hallways]]>><<unset $autismDiscloseTarget>><<endevent>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Disclose Teacher Brief
<<effects>>
<<set _teacher to $autismDiscloseTarget>>
<<run autismSetDisclosureDepth(_teacher, "brief")>>
You hand over the hospital summary and explain that the last section lists the changes recommended for lessons.
<br><br>
<<switch _teacher>>
	<<case "Doren">>
		Doren reads the whole page slowly, including the parts you had hoped <<he>> might skip. When <<he>> reaches the recommendations, <<he>> takes out <<his>> planner.
		<br><br>
		"Thanks for showing me," <<he>> says. "You don't owe me the whole story. I can work from what Harper has written here."
		<br><br>
		<<He>> copies two notes onto the inside cover. "One instruction at a time when the task is complicated, and the important part written down as well. That's reasonable. Honestly, half the class will probably benefit from me doing it."
		<span class="green">Doren agrees to use the adjustments.</span>
	<<case "River">>
		River reads the recommendations, skips back to check one line, then hands the page back.
		<br><br>
		"Tell me what changes in my lesson."
		<br><br>
		You explain that you need instructions broken into smaller steps and written down when possible.
		<br><br>
		"Alright. I'll do that." <<He>> does not make a note of it, but during the next lesson the instructions appear on the board in shorter steps.
		River follows the adjustments <span class="green">without drawing attention to them</span>.
	<<case "Sirris">>
		"Of course, give it here," Sirris says, taking the sheet with both hands. <<He>> reads every line, and the concern on <<his>> face grows as <<he>> reaches the end.
		<br><br>
		"You should have told me you were struggling this much. Please, if a lesson becomes too much, come to me. I mean that."
		<br><br>
		<<He>> says the last part loudly, and two students still packing their bags turn towards you.
		Sirris agrees to help, but <span class="teal">draws attention to the conversation</span>.
	<<default>>
		Winter reads the recommendations twice before handing the page back.
		<br><br>
		<<if C.npc.Winter.love gte 30 and $delinquency lt setup.schoolDiscipline.slipThreshold>>
			"I can give instructions in stages and allow you time to answer before I move on," <<he>> says. "In return, I expect you to use that time to keep up with the work."
			Winter <span class="green">agrees to the adjustments</span> and expects you to keep up with the work.
		<<elseif autismSchoolPlan()>>
			"The office has already sent me this," <<he>> says. "You did not need to bring me another copy."
			<br><br>
			<<He>> returns the sheet. "I will follow the listed adjustments because that is now school procedure. Do not mistake that for an invitation to negotiate the rest of my lessons."
			Winter follows the written plan, though <<he>> <span class="teal">offers nothing beyond it</span>.
		<<else>>
			"What, specifically, do you expect me to change?" <<he>> asks.
			<br><br>
			You point to the recommendations. <<He>> writes them into <<his>> planner, though <<his>> expression makes it clear that <<he>> would prefer the request to come through the school office.
			Winter records the request, but does so <span class="teal">reluctantly</span>.
		<</if>>
<</switch>>
<<run autismDiscloseTo(_teacher)>>
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Hallways]]>><<unset $autismDiscloseTarget>><<endevent>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Disclose Teacher Full
<<effects>>
<<set _teacher to $autismDiscloseTarget>>
<<run autismSetDisclosureDepth(_teacher, "full")>>
You explain what tends to go wrong during lessons. Several instructions given together can become difficult to hold in your head, especially if the room is already noisy. Crowded corridors can leave you tired before the next class starts, and being called on without warning is difficult when you are still working out the previous instruction.
<br><br>
You keep going until you have given all the examples you can think of.
<br><br>
<<switch _teacher>>
	<<case "Doren">>
		Doren listens without interrupting, then turns <<his>> planner around and points to a blank space.
		<br><br>
		"The questions in front of the class are mine," <<he>> says. "I do that because I want everyone involved, but if you're still processing the last thing I said, calling your name isn't helping."
		<br><br>
		You suggest warning you before the lesson if <<he>> plans to ask you something. Doren writes it down.
		<br><br>
		"I'll forget sometimes," <<he>> says. "If I do, tell me afterwards. I'd rather be corrected than keep doing something that makes the lesson harder for you."
		Doren understands what you asked for <span class="green">without treating you differently</span> in every other way.<<stress -5>><<lstress>>
	<<case "River">>
		River listens with <<his>> arms folded, waiting until you finish before answering.
		<br><br>
		"So I stop throwing questions at you without warning, I split long instructions, and I put the important steps where you can read them," <<he>> says. "That's what you need from me?"
		<br><br>
		You confirm it. <<He>> nods. "Then that's what I'll change."
		River focuses on the practical changes and <span class="green">agrees to use them</span>.
	<<case "Sirris">>
		Sirris listens with growing concern, then reaches out and puts a hand on your shoulder even though you have just explained that unexpected touch can make things worse.
		<br><br>
		"Oh, you poor thing. You've been trying to manage all of that in class without telling anyone?"
		<br><br>
		<<He>> immediately starts offering extra time, a quieter seat, help after lessons, and several other things you did not ask for. By the time you leave, <<he>> clearly means well, but you keep thinking about how much of yourself you ended up explaining.
		Sirris offers plenty of support, but <span class="teal">you feel uncomfortable about how much you shared</span>.<<stress 4>><<gstress>>
	<<default>>
		Winter hears you out without interrupting.
		<br><br>
		<<if C.npc.Winter.love gte 30 and $delinquency lt setup.schoolDiscipline.slipThreshold>>
			"You have told me more than the report required," <<he>> says. "That was useful."
			<br><br>
			<<He>> taps <<his>> pen against the note about questions. "I call on students without warning because it keeps the room awake. I can find another way to make sure you are following the lesson."
			<br><br>
			Winter writes the change down and closes the planner.
			Winter understands and <span class="green">agrees to change how <<he>> asks you questions</span>.<<stress -4>><<lstress>>
		<<else>>
			"I understand what you are describing," <<he>> says after you finish.
			<br><br>
			<<He>> closes the report. "I will act on the recommendations because they concern my teaching. The rest is personal information you chose to share, and I will not turn it into a continuing discussion."
			<br><br>
			Winter makes it clear that <<he>> will use the adjustments but does not want to discuss your personal experiences further.
			Winter will use the adjustments, but <span class="teal">does not want a more personal discussion</span>.<<stress 4>><<gstress>>
		<</if>>
<</switch>>
<<run autismDiscloseTo(_teacher)>>
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Hallways]]>><<unset $autismDiscloseTarget>><<endevent>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Lesson Stall
<<set $outside to 0>><<set $location to "school">><<schooleffects>><<effects>>
<<set _teacher to $autismLessonTeacher>>
<<set _knows to autismTeacherKnows(_teacher)>>
<<set _deep to autismDisclosureDepth(_teacher) is "full">>
<<switch _teacher>>
	<<case "Sirris">><<npc Sirris>>
	<<case "Doren">><<npc Doren>>
	<<case "Winter">><<npc Winter>>
	<<default>><<npc River>>
<</switch>><<person1>>
<<run autismSchoolDayAdd("stall")>>
The task is written on the board, and everyone around you has already started.
<br><br>
You read the instructions again. You know what the words mean and you know how to do the work, but your pen is still beside the page. You tell yourself to pick it up, then keep sitting there while the class moves ahead.
<br><br>
The longer you wait, the more attention you give to the fact that you have not started. That makes reaching for the pen harder instead of easier.
<br><br>
<<print autismColdStartLine()>>
<br><br>
<<if _knows>>
	Your teacher notices.
	<br><br>
	<<switch _teacher>>
		<<case "Doren">>
			Doren comes over without saying your name across the room. <<He>> crouches beside the desk and points to the first line.
			<br><br>
			"Forget the rest for now. Write the date, then copy the first number. I'll come back after you've had a few minutes with that."
			<br><br>
			You write the date, then the number. With the pen already in your hand, moving it to the first question takes less effort, and you begin writing.
			<span class="green">Started.</span><<stress -6>><<lstress>><<run autismRestBreak(4)>>
		<<case "River">>
			River stops beside your desk without turning towards you. "Number the questions one to six down the margin. Don't answer them yet."
			<br><br>
			<<He>> keeps walking. You write the six numbers, and by the time you reach the bottom your hand is already moving. You return to the first line and begin the answer without anyone else noticing why River stopped.
			<span class="green">Started, with no audience.</span><<stress -5>><<lstress>><<run autismRestBreak(4)>>
		<<case "Sirris">>
			Sirris notices from the front of the room. "Are you stuck, dear? That's alright, I'll come and help."
			<br><br>
			Several students look up as <<he>> crosses the room. <<He>> points out the first step and waits beside you while you do it, then stays for the next one as well. You start working, though you keep noticing <<him>> standing beside your desk.
			Started. <span class="teal">Everybody knows you needed help to start</span>.<<stress -2>><<llstress>><<run autismRestBreak(2)>>
		<<default>>
			Winter stops at your desk. "Tell me what is stopping you."
			<br><br>
			You explain that you understand the task. You simply have not managed to begin it.
			<br><br>
			<<if _deep or C.npc.Winter.love gte 30>>
				Winter takes a sheet of scrap paper and covers the lower half of the page, leaving only the first question visible.
				<br><br>
				"Then stop looking at the whole task. Do the question you can see, and uncover the next one when you finish."
				<br><br>
				You read the first question again and begin writing.
				<span class="green">Started, briskly.</span><<stress -4>><<lstress>><<run autismRestBreak(3)>>
			<<else>>
				<<He>> glances at the note from the office, then points to the first question. "Begin there. I will come back before the end of the lesson and check that you have moved on."
				<br><br>
				The instruction gives you one immediate action to follow. You put your pen to the page and start.
				<span class="teal">Started, under supervision you did not enjoy.</span><<stress -2>><<llstress>><<run autismRestBreak(2)>>
			<</if>>
	<</switch>>
	<br><br>
	<<set _autismNext to ($autismLessonReturn || "Hallways")>><<link "Next" _autismNext>><<unset $autismLessonReturn, $autismLessonTeacher, $autismLessonScene>><<endevent>><</link>>
	<br>
<<else>>
	Nobody notices. From the outside, you are simply sitting quietly with an open book while everyone else works.
	<br><br>
	<<link [[Break it into one tiny first action|Autism Lesson Stall Small]]>><</link>>
	<br>
	<<link [[Ask for the first step|Autism Lesson Stall Ask]]>><</link>>
	<br>
	<<link [[Force it|Autism Lesson Stall Force]]>><</link>>
	<br>
	<<link [[Stare at it until the lesson ends|Autism Lesson Stall Wait]]>><</link>>
	<br>
<</if>>

:: Autism Lesson Stall Small
<<set $outside to 0>><<set $location to "school">><<schooleffects>><<effects>>
You stop telling yourself to begin the whole task. Instead, you pick up the pen and write the date.
<br><br>
Once the date is there, you write the first question number underneath it. Your hand is already moving, so you copy the first part of the question and begin working through it.
<br><br>
You have spent eleven minutes trying to start. Now that you have made the first few marks, you can continue.
<<stress -4>><<lstress>><<run autismRestBreak(3)>>
<br><br>
<<set _autismNext to ($autismLessonReturn || "Hallways")>><<link "Next" _autismNext>><<unset $autismLessonReturn, $autismLessonTeacher, $autismLessonScene>><<endevent>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Lesson Stall Ask
<<set $outside to 0>><<set $location to "school">><<schooleffects>><<effects>>
<<set _teacher to $autismLessonTeacher>>
<<switch _teacher>>
	<<case "Sirris">><<npc Sirris>>
	<<case "Doren">><<npc Doren>>
	<<case "Winter">><<npc Winter>>
	<<default>><<npc River>>
<</switch>><<person1>>
You put your hand up and ask what you should do first.
<br><br>
The first instruction is already on the board. You have read it several times, which makes asking embarrassing, but reading the same line again has not helped you begin.
<br><br>
<<if _teacher is "Winter" and C.npc.Winter.love lt 30>>
	Winter looks from you to the board. "The first instruction is written directly in front of you. Read it to me."
	<br><br>
	You read it aloud. <<He>> waits until you finish. "Then do only that part. If you still cannot continue afterwards, ask again."
	<br><br>
	You turn back to the page with several students still looking your way.
	<<stress 6>><<gstress>><<run autismSocialDemand(2)>>
<<elseif _teacher is "Sirris">>
	Sirris comes straight over. "Of course. We can do it together."
	<br><br>
	<<He>> reads the first instruction, waits while you complete it, then reads the next one as well. The help gets you moving, though <<his>> attention stays on you longer than you wanted.
	<<stress -2>><<llstress>><<run autismRestBreak(2)>>
<<else>>
	<<switch _teacher>>
		<<case "Doren">>
			Doren walks over and lowers <<his>> voice. "You already know what it says, don't you? Alright. Let's make it smaller."
			<br><br>
			<<He>> points to the first action in the sentence. "Do only that part. Don't worry about the rest until your pen's moving."
		<<case "River">>
			River reads the instruction once, then separates it into two directions. "Do the first part now. Leave the second until you've finished."
		<<default>>
			Winter walks to your desk and points at the first line. "Ignore everything after this sentence. Complete this part, then read the next one."
	<</switch>>
	<br><br>
	The single action gives you somewhere to begin. You put your pen to the page and start.
	<<stress -4>><<lstress>><<run autismRestBreak(3)>>
<</if>>
<br><br>
<<set _autismNext to ($autismLessonReturn || "Hallways")>><<link "Next" _autismNext>><<unset $autismLessonReturn, $autismLessonTeacher, $autismLessonScene>><<endevent>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Lesson Stall Force
<<set $outside to 0>><<set $location to "school">><<schooleffects>><<effects>>
You grip the pen and force your hand to make the first mark.
<br><br>
Once you begin, you can do the work normally. The effort of forcing yourself to start leaves you tense and tired, even though the task itself is not difficult.
<br><br>
You keep working, but you already know the rest of the afternoon will be harder.
<<stress 5>><<gstress>><<run autismSocialDemand(1)>>
<br><br>
<<set _autismNext to ($autismLessonReturn || "Hallways")>><<link "Next" _autismNext>><<unset $autismLessonReturn, $autismLessonTeacher, $autismLessonScene>><<endevent>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Lesson Stall Wait
<<set $outside to 0>><<set $location to "school">><<schooleffects>><<effects>>
You stop trying to force yourself to begin.
<br><br>
You remain at the desk with the task open in front of you. You keep thinking about starting, then looking at the first line again, but your hand never reaches for the pen. The entire lesson passes this way.
<br><br>
The bell goes. You put the untouched page in your bag.
<<stress 4>><<gstress>>
<<if $autismLessonTeacher is "Winter" and !autismTeacherKnows("Winter")>>
	<br>
	Winter notes the blank page on <<his>> way past the desks and says nothing, which is worse.
	<<delinquency 2.5>><<ggdelinquency>>
<</if>>
<br><br>
<<set _autismNext to ($autismLessonReturn || "Hallways")>><<link "Next" _autismNext>><<unset $autismLessonReturn, $autismLessonTeacher, $autismLessonScene>><<endevent>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Corridor Strain
<<set $outside to 0>><<set $location to "school">><<schooleffects>><<effects>>
<<run autismSchoolDayAdd("corridor")>>
The bell goes and the corridor fills.
<br><br>
<<if autismSpectrumPhase() is "burnout">>
	The sounds stop separating out. You can hear that people are talking, but not which words belong to whom,
	or whether any of them are meant for you.
<<else>>
	Four hundred people move at once, all of them making noise. Somebody says your name, or a word close enough
	to it that you turn your head. A locker slams shut two feet from your ear.
<</if>>
<br><br>
You have five minutes to reach your next lesson, which means you have to stay in the crowded corridor until you get there.
<br><br>
<<if autismMayStepOut()>>
	The infirmary is the agreed place. <span class="gold">You are allowed to go there</span>.
	<br>
	<<link [[Go to the infirmary (0:10)|Autism Corridor Quiet]]>><<pass 10>><</link>>
	<br>
<</if>>
<<link [[Find a corner and wait it out (0:05)|Autism Corridor Corner]]>><<pass 5>><</link>>
<br>
<<link [[Push through it|Autism Corridor Push]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Corridor Quiet
<<set $outside to 0>><<set $location to "school">><<schooleffects>><<effects>>
<<run autismSchoolDayAdd("quietroom")>>
You go to the infirmary and say you need the room.
<br><br>
<<if $per_npc.school_nurse>><<loadNPC 0 school_nurse>><<person1>>
	The nurse looks up, sees which of you it is, and does not ask anything else.
	<<if autismHasPreference("space")>>
		<<He>> gestures at the far bed, the one behind the curtain, and stays where <<he>> is.
	<<elseif autismHasPreference("quiet")>>
		<<He>> pulls the curtain and turns the desk lamp off on <<his>> way past.
	<<elseif autismHasPreference("plain")>>
		"Bed. Ten minutes. I will tell you when." Nothing else.
	<<else>>
		<<He>> nods you through to the beds.
	<</if>>
<<else>>
	The room is empty. You take the bed at the back and pull the curtain.
<</if>>
<br><br>
You can still hear the corridor through the wall, but the sound is much quieter here.
<br><br>
You sit for ten minutes with your hands on your knees. After a while, you can finish one thought without losing it when another sound reaches you.
<<stress -10>><<lstress>><<run autismRestBreak(8)>>
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Hallways]]>><<endevent>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Corridor Corner
<<set $outside to 0>><<set $location to "school">><<schooleffects>><<effects>>
You move into the narrow space between the lockers and the fire door, then turn so the wall is behind you.
<br><br>
The wall blocks the noise from behind you. It still comes from everywhere else.
<br><br>
<<if autismMayStepOut()>>
	You could have gone to the infirmary. You think about that for the whole five minutes.
<<else>>
	<span class="red">You are late to your next lesson</span>, and standing in a corridor is not a reason anyone accepts.
	<<delinquency 2>><<gdelinquency>>
<</if>>
<<stress -3>><<lstress>><<run autismRestBreak(2)>>
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Hallways]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Corridor Push
<<set $outside to 0>><<set $location to "school">><<schooleffects>><<effects>>
You keep walking through the crowd and focus on reaching the classroom.
<br><br>
You arrive on time. Your jaw hurts because you have been clenching your teeth, and your hands keep moving under the desk. Nobody comments because you made it to class as expected.
<<stress 6>><<gstress>><<run autismSocialDemand(2)>>
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Hallways]]>><<endevent>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Cold Start
<<set _kind to $autismColdKind || "task">>
<<switch _kind>>
	<<case "study">>
		The books have been open on the desk for twenty minutes.
		<br><br>
		You know which chapter. You know roughly how long it will take, and you have done it before without
		any trouble. Your hands stay where they are.
	<<case "chores">>
		One job. Eleven minutes of work.
		<br><br>
		You have walked past it three times now. Each time you thought "I should do that," in those exact
		words, and each time you kept walking.
	<<case "work">>
		The shift has started. Nobody had to tell you what the first task is, because you worked it out
		on your way in.
		<br><br>
		You know what the first task is, but you remain standing in the same place. You tell yourself to move towards it, and nothing happens.
	<<case "service">>
		They handed you the list and pointed down the corridor. You know exactly what to do.
		<br><br>
		You remain in the doorway with the list in your hand. After a while, the supervisor behind you starts watching you.
	<<default>>
		Everything you need is ready in front of you. You know what to do next, but you still cannot make yourself begin.
<</switch>>
<br><br>
<<print autismColdStartLine()>>
<br><br>
<<link [[Do one tiny piece of it (0:05)|Autism Cold Start Small]]>><<pass 5>><</link>>
<br>
<<link [[Run the usual sequence and let it carry you (0:10)|Autism Cold Start Routine]]>><<pass 10>><</link>>
<br>
<<link [[Force yourself into it (0:05)|Autism Cold Start Force]]>><<pass 5>><</link>>
<br>
<<link [[Give up on it for now|Autism Cold Start Abandon]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Cold Start Small
<<effects>>
<<set _back to ensureAutismSpectrumState().coldReturn || "Bedroom">>
You choose the smallest action you can do without thinking about the whole task.
<br><br>
You straighten the page, write the heading, or open the drawer you need. Once your hands are moving, you continue with the next small action until you are doing the actual work.
<<stress -4>><<lstress>><<run autismRestBreak(3)>><<autism-cold-cost true>>
<br><br>
<<link "Next" _back>><<run delete $autismSpectrum.coldReturn>><<run delete $autismSpectrum.coldCost>><<unset $autismColdKind>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Cold Start Routine
<<effects>>
<<set _back to ensureAutismSpectrumState().coldReturn || "Bedroom">>
You begin with the same routine you normally use before this task.
<br><br>
You do the first three steps in their usual order. When the routine reaches the part where the work starts, you continue without stopping to decide whether to begin.
<br><br>
The routine takes ten minutes, but it makes starting much easier than forcing yourself through it.
<<stress -3>><<lstress>><<run autismRestBreak(3)>><<autism-cold-cost true>>
<br><br>
<<link "Next" _back>><<run delete $autismSpectrum.coldReturn>><<run delete $autismSpectrum.coldCost>><<unset $autismColdKind>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Cold Start Force
<<effects>>
<<set _back to ensureAutismSpectrumState().coldReturn || "Bedroom">>
You force yourself to make the first movement and begin.
<br><br>
The task gets started, but the effort leaves you tense and tired. Even a short job can take a lot out of you when you have to force yourself through the first step, and the tiredness stays with you afterwards.
<<stress 6>><<gstress>><<autism-cold-cost true>>
<br><br>
<<link "Next" _back>><<run delete $autismSpectrum.coldReturn>><<run delete $autismSpectrum.coldCost>><<unset $autismColdKind>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Cold Start Abandon
<<effects>>
<<set _back to ensureAutismSpectrumState().coldReturn || "Bedroom">>
You leave it.
<br><br>
The job remains unfinished. You remember it while eating, while walking, and every time you pass the room. Even while doing something else, part of your attention keeps returning to it.
<<autism-cold-cost false>>
<br><br>
<<link "Next" _back>><<run delete $autismSpectrum.coldReturn>><<run delete $autismSpectrum.coldCost>><<unset $autismColdKind>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Kylar Quiet
<<effects>>
Kylar has been talking about the old drainage system beneath the town for more than twenty minutes. <<He>> has been reading about it for the last eleven days and keeps finding more details to explain.
<br><br>
You keep doing your own activity while <<he>> talks. Every so often one of you says something, and the other gives a short reply without stopping what they are doing.
<br><br>
After a while, you notice that you have not been checking whether your expression looks interested. You have simply been sitting with <<him>>.
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Autism Kylar Quiet 2]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Kylar Quiet 2
<<effects>>
Kylar stops halfway through a sentence and looks over at you.
<br><br>
"Is this alright?" <<He>> asks. "This. Me going on. You've not said anything for ages and I can't
tell if that's the good kind or the kind where I've done something."
<br><br>
You tell <<him>> it is the good kind.
<br><br>
Kylar lets out a long breath and lowers <<his>> shoulders.
<br><br>
"It's just—" <<He>> stops and starts again. "With most people, I keep trying to work out how long I'm allowed to talk before they get bored. I get it wrong. Then they start pretending they're still interested, and I can't stop thinking about that instead of what I was saying."
<br><br>
<<He>> looks at the floor. "I don't do that here. I hadn't noticed until just now that I don't do it here."
<br><br>
<<link [[Say you do the same thing|Autism Kylar Quiet Same]]>><</link>>
<br>
<<link [[Say nothing|Autism Kylar Quiet Silence]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Kylar Quiet Same
<<effects>>
You tell <<him>> that you do something similar. You watch how long you have been speaking, check your expression, and try to judge when a pause has lasted too long.
<br><br>
Kylar lifts <<his>> head so quickly that <<his>> hair falls away from <<his>> face. "You do that too? The thing with the pause?"
<br><br>
<<He>> leans closer. "How do you know when it stops being a normal silence and starts being the kind where the other person thinks you're strange?"
<br><br>
You tell <<him>> you do not know. Sometimes you count.
<br><br>
"I count as well," <<he>> says. <<His>> voice becomes quieter. "I thought I'd made that up. I thought it was just one of my stupid rules."
<br><br>
Neither of you finds a better answer, but Kylar keeps sitting beside you without trying to fill the silence. Knowing <<he>> checks some of the same things makes it easier to stop checking yourself so closely while you are with <<him>>.
<<stress -6>><<lstress>><<npcincr Kylar love 2>><<glove>>
<br><br>
<<set _autismNext to ($autismSpectrum.kylarQuietReturn || "Kylar Basement")>><<link "Next" _autismNext>><<run delete $autismSpectrum.kylarQuietReturn>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Kylar Quiet Silence
<<effects>>
You do not answer. Kylar does not ask again or try to fill the silence.
<br><br>
After a while, <<he>> returns to talking about the drainage system from where <<he>> stopped. <<He>> treats the long pause as normal and does not expect you to explain it.
<br><br>
You relax a little when you realise you can stay quiet without <<him>> thinking something is wrong.
<<stress -6>><<lstress>><<npcincr Kylar love 1>><<glove>>
<br><br>
<<set _autismNext to ($autismSpectrum.kylarQuietReturn || "Kylar Basement")>><<link "Next" _autismNext>><<run delete $autismSpectrum.kylarQuietReturn>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Service Stall
They hand you the list and point down the corridor.
<br><br>
The jobs are simple: wipe the tables, stack the chairs, then take the bags to the bins. You read the list once and already
know what order you would do them in. The whole job should take about forty minutes.
<br><br>
You are still standing in the doorway with the list in your hand.
<br><br>
<<print autismColdStartLine()>>
<br><br>
The supervising <<person>> waits while you stay there. After about a minute and a half, <<he>> says, "Any time you like."
<br><br>
<<if autismSchoolPlan() or autismDisclosedTo("nurse")>>
	<<He>> checks the note on <<his>> clipboard again, then points to the first item on your list.
	<br><br>
	"Tables first. Just the tables. Come and find me when they're done."
	<br><br>
	One clear job is easier to begin than the full list. You take the cloth and start on the nearest table.
	<<stress -4>><<lstress>><<run autismRestBreak(3)>>
<<else>>
	You want to explain that you are not refusing to work. You cannot organise the explanation before <<he>> writes something on the clipboard.
	<br><br>
	A little later, you manage to start without another instruction. You work quickly for the rest of the session,
	but the note on the clipboard was already written.
	<<stress 8>><<ggstress>><<run autismSocialDemand(3)>>
<</if>>
<br><br>
<<set _autismNext to ($autismServiceReturn || "School Service Work")>><<link "Next" _autismNext>><<unset $autismServiceReturn>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Suspension Day
<<run autismSchoolDayAdd("suspension")>>
The house is empty, and you have no lessons or appointments today.
<br><br>
You expected the free day to be easier.
<br><br>
<<set _obligations to autismObligationLabels()>>
<<if _obligations.length>>
	Without bells or lessons deciding what happens next, you have to choose when to begin every activity yourself. You keep thinking about the unfinished tasks you still need to deal with.
	<<for _ob range _obligations>>
		| <<print _ob.toUpperFirst()>>
	<</for>>
	<br><br>
	You move between rooms and keep thinking about the list, but you do not begin any of it. By the afternoon, you are already tired despite having completed very little.
<<else>>
	There are no urgent jobs waiting, but choosing what to do is still difficult without the usual school schedule.
	<br><br>
	You think about three different things you would like to do. You keep changing your mind between them until enough time passes that you start none of them.
<</if>>
<br><br>
<<set _autismNext to ($autismSuspensionReturn || "Bedroom")>><<link "Next" _autismNext>><<unset $autismSuspensionReturn>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism School Day
<<set _beats to ensureAutismSpectrumState().schoolDayBeats || []>>
<<run autismSchoolDayAdd("reckoning")>>
You get through the front door and stop in the hall. You know you need to decide where to go next, but after the school day
even that small choice feels like more work.
<br><br>
<<if _beats.includes("stall") and _beats.includes("corridor")>>
	You think back over how the day built up. It started with twenty minutes of sitting in front of work you understood
	without being able to begin it. Between lessons, the noise and movement in the corridors took more effort out of you.
	Neither event lasted very long, but dealing with both before the rest of the school day left you worn out.
<<elseif _beats.includes("stall")>>
	The first difficult part was near the start of the day. You spent twenty minutes unable to begin work you already
	understood, and recovering from that effort made the later lessons harder.
<<elseif _beats.includes("corridor")>>
	The corridors are what you keep thinking about. Each trip only lasted a few minutes, but the noise, people and movement
	forced you to keep adjusting where you walked and what you paid attention to. By the last lesson, those trips had taken
	more out of you than you expected.
<<else>>
	Nothing unusual happened at school today. You still arrive home tired, which makes it harder to blame the feeling on
	one bad lesson or one difficult event.
<</if>>
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Autism School Day 2]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism School Day 2
<<effects>>
<<set _beats to ensureAutismSpectrumState().schoolDayBeats || []>>
<<set _phase to autismSpectrumPhase()>>
<<set _mask to autismMaskingLevel()>>
<<if _mask gte 55>>
	Since this morning, you have been checking your expression, your voice and when you speak before other people can notice
	anything unusual. Standing in the hall, you realise you are still doing it automatically.
<<elseif _beats.includes("quietroom")>>
	You spent ten minutes behind the infirmary curtain at lunch, away from the busiest parts of school. The afternoon was
	still tiring, but you had more patience and concentration after that break than you had before it.
<</if>>
<br><br>
<<if _phase is "burnout">>
	<span class="red">You are burnt out</span>, and there is still an evening to get through.
<<elseif _phase is "strained">>
	You are strained, but <span class="gold">the school day is over</span>.
<<else>>
	<span class="green">You are alright after a full school day</span>, and you notice the difference.
<</if>>
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Orphanage]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Checkup
<<set $autismCheckupTopic to autismCheckupTopic()>>
<<set _h to autismHistory()>>
Harper has your file open before you arrive. <<He>> has already read the latest notes.
<br><br>
<<switch $autismCheckupTopic>>
	<<case "overload">>
		"Start with the recent episode," <<he>> says. "<<if autismLastOverloadForm() is "shutdown">>The notes say you
		stopped answering people<<else>>The notes say you could no longer continue what you were doing<</if>>. Tell me what was happening
		from your side."
	<<case "burnout">>
		Harper looks at you before checking the file. "You look exhausted. How long have you been this worn out?"
	<<case "burnoutPast">>
		"You have been through burnout since I last saw you," <<he>> says. "You are doing better now. Tell me what changed
		while you recovered."
	<<case "masking">>
		"When I opened the door, you changed your expression before you spoke to me," Harper says. "How often have you been
		checking your face, voice or replies like that this week?"
	<<case "coldstarts">>
		"Something specific came up this week," <<he>> says, checking the page. "Starting tasks. You knew how to do them,
		but getting through the first action was difficult. It happened <<print _h.coldStarts>> times that I know about."
	<<case "backlog">>
		"There are unfinished things on your list," Harper says. "I am interested in what having all of them unfinished
		is doing to your stress and concentration."
	<<case "disclosure">>
		<<He>> taps the file. "You have had the report for a while and nobody at school has seen it. Have you chosen to
		keep it private, or have you kept postponing the decision?"
	<<case "accommodations">>
		"The school has the report," <<he>> says. "Has anything changed in your lessons, or did the office only record it
		and leave everything else the same?"
	<<case "composure">>
		"You have been rehearsing expressions and replies before difficult conversations," Harper says. "You did it before
		you came in today as well. Has it been helping?"
	<<case "regulation">>
		"You have been stimming and taking breaks from masking somewhere safe," <<he>> says. "I want to know what actually
		helps and what you are only doing because somebody suggested it."
	<<default>>
		"<<print autismHyperfixationName().toUpperFirst()>>," Harper says, reading the name from the page.
		"Still interested in it? Tell me about it. You have spent several appointments talking about problems, and I want
		to hear about something you enjoy as well."
<</switch>>
<br><br>
<<link [[Answer honestly|Autism Checkup Honest]]>><</link>>
<br>
<<link [[Downplay it|Autism Checkup Downplay]]>><</link>>
<br>
<<link [[Say you do not know how to answer that|Autism Checkup Unsure]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Checkup Honest
<<effects>>
You give <<him>> the version you usually leave out.
<br><br>
<<switch $autismCheckupTopic>>
	<<case "overload">>
		<<if autismLastOverloadForm() is "shutdown">>
			You explain that you could still hear what people were saying. You understood that they wanted an answer, but you could not turn the answer in your head into speech.
			<br><br>
			Harper stops writing and looks up. "That distinction matters. Somebody watching from the outside may assume you stopped understanding because you stopped replying. You were still hearing them and trying to respond."
		<<else>>
			You describe what happened when the overload became visible: the sounds you made, the movements you could not stop, and how aware you were of people watching afterwards.
			<br><br>
			"You did not decide to react that way," Harper says. "If you have spent the week blaming yourself for losing control, include what was happening before it reached that point. I need the build-up as well as the part everybody saw."
		<</if>>
	<<case "burnout">>
		You say you cannot remember exactly when it started. The last several days have felt too similar, and you have stopped keeping track of which day first became this difficult.
		<br><br>
		Harper writes that down without asking you to guess. "That tells me more than an invented date would. If the days have blurred together, I want that recorded as part of the problem."
	<<case "burnoutPast">>
		You think through the recovery. You rested more, spent some time on something you actually wanted to do, and had fewer demands during the week.
		<br><br>
		Harper underlines the last part. "You improved when there were fewer demands. Keep that in mind before the next difficult week. Reducing the load early may help more than waiting until you can no longer manage it."
	<<case "masking">>
		You explain how often you check yourself: your expression, the timing of your replies, the words you use when you do not know what to say, and how often you look at the other person so they do not think you are ignoring them.
		<br><br>
		Harper adds several notes before answering. "Most people around you will never see that work, but your body still pays for it. I am not asking you to stop everywhere. I want to know where you can safely stop checking yourself for part of the day."
	<<case "coldstarts">>
		You explain that the task itself is usually clear. The difficulty is making the first movement towards it: opening the book, putting the pen down, or writing the first line.
		<br><br>
		Harper nods. "From the outside, that can look like refusal. Somebody sees you sitting there and assumes you do not want to do the task."
		<br><br>
		You tell <<him>> that happens often.
		<br><br>
		"Then we work on the first action instead of arguing about motivation," <<he>> says. "If opening the book is where you stop, we make opening the book the whole first step."
	<<case "backlog">>
		You explain that you have not forgotten the unfinished tasks. You remember them repeatedly throughout the day, even while doing something else, and each reminder adds more stress.
		<br><br>
		Harper makes a note beside the list. "People around you only see unfinished work. They do not see you remembering it twenty times while still being unable to begin. That difference matters when we decide what support is useful."
	<<case "disclosure">>
		You admit that you never made a clear decision. You kept putting it off, and after a while keeping the report private became what happened by default.
		<br><br>
		"There is no deadline for telling people," Harper says. "But postponing the decision still has a result: nobody can use the report to support you while they do not know it exists. I only want you to recognise that as a choice you are still making, even if you have not said yes or no."
	<<case "accommodations">>
		You tell <<him>> what has changed and what has stayed the same.
		<<if autismSchoolPlan()>>
			The office recorded the adjustments. Some teachers follow them without comment, while another makes the adjustment obvious to everybody around you.
		<<else>>
			Only one or two people know, so those people are the only places where anything has changed.
		<</if>>
		<br><br>
		Harper listens, then asks which change actually helped. "The paperwork only tells staff what they are allowed to do differently. Whether it works depends on what each person does with it in the room."
	<<case "composure">>
		You explain how you rehearse before difficult conversations: expressions in the mirror, the timing of when to speak, and two or three replies you can use while thinking of a real answer.
		<br><br>
		Harper asks whether the preparation makes the conversation easier once it starts.
		<br><br>
		You say that it can, but preparing everything is tiring. Some conversations become easier afterwards, while on other days the preparation takes more energy than it saves.
		<br><br>
		"Then it is useful in some situations and expensive in others," <<he>> says. "Use it when the preparation saves more effort than it costs you."
	<<case "regulation">>
		You say the breaks and stimming usually help. Some days you do not notice an immediate change, but forcing yourself to stop feels worse.
		<br><br>
		Harper writes that down. "You do not need to prove that every break produces a dramatic improvement. If the alternative is making yourself more distressed by staying still, that is useful information on its own."
	<<default>>
		You talk about the interest properly because <<he>> asked. You explain the parts you usually cut short when you notice another person's attention fading, but Harper keeps listening and does not look at the clock.
		<br><br>
		When you finally stop, <<he>> closes the pen. "You spoke much more freely just then than you usually do in here. Pay attention to where that happens during the week. I want to know which places leave you with more energy instead of taking it."
<</switch>>
<<stress -8>><<lstress>><<run autismRestBreak(5)>>
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Autism Checkup End]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Checkup Downplay
<<effects>>
You say it was fine and manageable, even if the notes make it sound worse.
<br><br>
Harper waits instead of replying. After several seconds of silence, you start thinking through the week again.
<br><br>
"You may be right," <<he>> says. "You were there, and I was not. I have noticed that you often call things 'fine' here, then tell me the next week that they caused more trouble than you first said."
<br><br>
<<He>> makes a short note. "I am not going to argue you out of it. I am going to write down that you said it,
and we will both look at that in a month."
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Autism Checkup End]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Checkup Unsure
<<effects>>
You say you do not know how to answer. You try to think of one explanation for the whole week, but nothing clear comes to mind.
<br><br>
"That is an answer," Harper says. "It is a better one than most of what I get in here."
<br><br>
<<He>> puts the pen down. "Try it in pieces. Was it worse than the week before, better, or about the same?"
<br><br>
You can answer that question. Harper follows it with a few more specific ones, and you answer those as well. By the end, you have explained the week without needing one large answer.
<br><br>
"Breaking the question into smaller parts helped," <<he>> says. "We can do the same thing when a task feels too large to begin."<<stress -6>><<lstress>>
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Autism Checkup End]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Checkup End
<<set $outside to 0>><<set $location to "hospital">><<effects>>
Harper closes the file and gives you one thing to try before the next appointment.
<br><br>
<<switch $autismCheckupTopic>>
	<<case "overload" "burnout" "burnoutPast">>
		"Reduce the number of demands in the week where you can. I know you do not control all of them, but trying to
		manage the same overloaded schedule more efficiently will still leave you overloaded."
	<<case "masking" "composure">>
		"Choose one place where you can stop checking your expression, eye contact and replies every day. One reliable
		place is more useful than trying to change everything at once."
	<<case "coldstarts">>
		"Make the first action very small. Open the book. Write the date. Put the clothes beside the washing machine.
		You are trying to make starting easier, so the first action should be simple."
	<<case "backlog">>
		"Start with the smallest unfinished task. Finishing one gives you one fewer thing to remember while you deal
		with the rest."
	<<case "disclosure" "accommodations">>
		"Choose one person who could actually help and show them the part they need. You do not have to tell the whole
		institution everything at the same time."
	<<case "regulation">>
		"Keep using the breaks or movements that help, including on days when the change is small."
	<<default>>
		"Protect some time for the interest. Do not only allow yourself that time after everything else is finished."
<</switch>>
<br><br>
<<if autismHasDocumentation() and !autismSchoolPlan()>>
	<<if autismPrefersPrivate()>>
		<<He>> does not bring up the school. You already told <<him>> that you want the report kept private, and <<he>>
		leaves the decision with you.
	<<elseif autismIdentity() is "embarrassed">>
		<<He>> pauses before you leave. "You still have not shown the report to anyone. I am not going to push you.
		Think about whether there is one person who would make things easier if they knew."
	<<else>>
		<<He>> pauses before you leave. "The report is still with you. You can decide later if you want somebody else
		to see it."
	<</if>>
	<br><br>
<</if>>
<<run autismHistoryReset()>>
<<unset $autismCheckupTopic>>
<<link [[Return to the foyer|Hospital Foyer]]>><<endevent>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Identity
<<set $outside to 0>><<set $location to "hospital">><<effects>>
Harper finishes writing, then asks one more question.
<br><br>
"How do you feel about the diagnosis itself?"
<br><br>
<<link [[It explains things. It is a relief|Autism Identity Set]]>><<set $autismIdentityChoice to "relief">><</link>>
<br>
<<link [[It is a label, and you would rather not have one|Autism Identity Set]]>><<set $autismIdentityChoice to "label">><</link>>
<br>
<<link [[It is a document that might get you adjustments|Autism Identity Set]]>><<set $autismIdentityChoice to "practical">><</link>>
<br>
<<link [[It is part of who you are|Autism Identity Set]]>><<set $autismIdentityChoice to "identity">><</link>>
<br>
<<link [[You are embarrassed by it|Autism Identity Set]]>><<set $autismIdentityChoice to "embarrassed">><</link>>
<br>
<<link [[You do not know yet|Autism Identity Set]]>><<set $autismIdentityChoice to "unsure">><</link>>
<br>
<<link [[You would rather nobody ever knew|Autism Identity Set]]>><<set $autismIdentityChoice to "private">><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Identity Set
<<set $outside to 0>><<set $location to "hospital">><<effects>>
<<run autismSetIdentity($autismIdentityChoice)>>
<<switch $autismIdentityChoice>>
	<<case "relief">>
		You say it is a relief.
		<br><br>
		"Then let yourself feel relieved," Harper says. "You have spent years dealing with these problems without knowing why they happened. Now you have an explanation that fits what we found."
	<<case "label">>
		You say you do not much like labels, and that having one does not make you feel better about anything.
		<br><br>
		"That's fine," <<he>> says. "You can use the diagnosis when you need support without making it important to your identity. You do not have to feel positive about the label itself."
	<<case "practical">>
		You say the diagnosis matters because the report can help you get adjustments.
		<br><br>
		"That's a practical way to use it," Harper says. "Keep the report for the situations where it helps you get what you need."
	<<case "identity">>
		You say autism feels like part of who you are. The description fits your experiences, and you would not want someone to remove that part of you.
		<br><br>
		Harper nods. "Some people feel that way, while others only think of autism as a diagnosis. You can decide what it means to you."
	<<case "embarrassed">>
		You say you feel embarrassed about the diagnosis even though you cannot give a clear reason.
		<br><br>
		"You have spent years being told that some of these difficulties happened because you were not trying hard enough," Harper says. "A diagnosis does not make those memories disappear."
		<br><br>
		"It may take time before you feel differently about it."
	<<case "private">>
		You say you would rather nobody ever found out, and that you are not certain you should have come.
		<br><br>
		"Then keep it private," Harper says. "The report stays in your medical record unless you choose to show it to someone outside the hospital."
		<br><br>
		"Getting assessed does not mean you have to tell other people. Those are separate decisions."
	<<default>>
		You say you do not know yet.
		<br><br>
		"That's fine," <<he>> says. "You only received the result today. You do not need to decide how you feel about it before you leave this room."
<</switch>>
<br><br>
<<unset $autismIdentityChoice>>
<<link [[Next|Doctor Harper Autism Now End]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Compose
<<set _autismState to ensureAutismSpectrumState()>>
<<set _back to _autismState.composeReturn || "Bedroom">>
<<effects>>
<<set _composed to autismCompose()>>
<<if !_composed>>
	You start running through it again, then realise you already prepared the same replies and expressions earlier.
	Going over them again will only make them harder to use naturally.
	<br><br>
	<span class="blue">You are already prepared.</span>
	<br><br>
	<<link "Next" _back>><<run delete $autismSpectrum.composeReturn>><</link>>
<<else>>
	You look at yourself in the mirror and start with your expression.
	<br><br>
	A full smile feels difficult to keep in place, so you practise a smaller one that people usually read as listening.
	You make the expression, relax your face, then make it again until you can do it without checking every part first.
	<br><br>
	<<link [[Next|Autism Compose 2]]>><</link>>
<</if>>
<br>

:: Autism Compose 2
<<set _autismState to ensureAutismSpectrumState()>>
<<set _back to _autismState.composeReturn || "Bedroom">>
<<effects>>
Next you practise when to speak. You imagine a few common questions and practise answering before the other person starts talking again.
<br><br>
You prepare two or three short replies you can use when you need another second to think of a proper answer.
<br><br>
Finally, you practise looking at somebody for a count of three before looking away, then looking back. The eye contact
does not help you understand the conversation, but you know other people notice when you avoid it completely.
<br><br>
After fifteen minutes, you are tired from checking your expression, eye contact, and replies so closely. At least you have a few answers prepared for the next difficult conversation.
<br><br>
<<link "Next" _back>><<run delete $autismSpectrum.composeReturn>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Assessment Questionnaire
<<set _back to ensureAutismSpectrumState().assessmentReturn || "Bedroom">>
<<effects>>
You find a flat surface and get the questionnaires out.
<br><br>
The questionnaires are harder than speaking to Harper. During the appointment, <<nnpc_he "Harper">> could explain a question if you became stuck. Here, each statement only has a row of boxes beside it, and you have to choose one yourself.
<br><br>
<i>I find it easy to work out what someone means when they do not say it.</i> You read the sentence several times. You are unsure what "easy" is supposed to mean or what you should compare your experience with.
<br><br>
You decide to choose the first answer that seems close enough, avoid changing earlier answers, and stop when the hour is nearly over.
It takes most of the hour. By the end, you are tired from trying to reduce complicated experiences to a few boxes.
<<run autismAssessmentAddSource("self")>><<stress 6>><<gstress>>
<br><br>
<<link "Next" _back>><<run delete $autismSpectrum.assessmentReturn>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Assessment Records
<<set $outside to 0>><<set $location to "school">><<schooleffects>><<effects>>
<<npc Leighton>><<person1>>
You take the school form to the office and explain that Harper wants information from your records.
<br><br>
Leighton reads the hospital heading, checks the questions on the back, then places the form beside <<his>> keyboard.
<br><br>
<<if $delinquency gte setup.schoolDiscipline.dutyThreshold>>
	"So the hospital wants the school's version of you as well," <<he>> says. "Then they are going to get the version that exists in our records, not a kinder one because you are standing here."
	<br><br>
	<<He>> spends several minutes filling in the form, keeping it turned towards <<him>> while <<he>> copies dates and notes from your file.
<<else>>
	"This is mostly attendance, behaviour, and whether staff have raised concerns," <<he>> says. "For once, a form that may take less time than the conversation around it."
	<br><br>
	<<He>> completes the boxes while you wait, then places the form with the school's outgoing post.
<</if>>
<<run autismAssessmentAddSource("school")>>
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Hallways]]>><<endevent>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Assessment Teacher
<<set $outside to 0>><<set $location to "school">><<schooleffects>><<effects>>
<<set _teacher to $autismAssessmentTeacher>>
<<switch _teacher>>
	<<case "Sirris">><<npc Sirris>>
	<<case "Doren">><<npc Doren>>
	<<case "Winter">><<npc Winter>>
	<<default>><<npc River>>
<</switch>><<person1>>
You catch your teacher at the end of the lesson and hand over Harper's form.
<br><br>
<<switch _teacher>>
	<<case "Doren">>
		Doren reads every question before answering.
		<br><br>
		"I have wondered about some of this," <<he>> says, then immediately looks uncomfortable. "Sorry. That sounded like I've been diagnosing you from the front of the classroom. I haven't."
		<br><br>
		<<He>> folds the form once and puts it in <<his>> planner. "I'll take it home and answer properly. Harper asked what I have actually seen, so that's what I'll give <<him>>."
	<<case "River">>
		River scans the form, finds the section marked for a teacher, and fills it in against the nearest cupboard door.
		<br><br>
		<<He>> checks the last box, signs it, and hands it back. "That's my section finished. Do you need anything else from me?"
		<br><br>
		You say no. <<He>> nods and leaves.
	<<case "Sirris">>
		"Oh, of course I'll help," Sirris says, taking the form before you have finished explaining it.
		<br><br>
		<<He>> starts asking how long the assessment has been going on and whether you are worried about the result while several students are still packing up nearby. <<He>> completes the form, but also mentions it to another member of staff on <<his>> way out.
		<<stress 4>><<gstress>>
	<<default>>
		Winter reads the first page, then looks at you over the top of it.
		<br><br>
		<<if C.npc.Winter.love gte 30>>
			"I will answer what I have personally observed," <<he>> says. "The form is asking for evidence, not whether I like you. That should make the task quite straightforward."
		<<else>>
			"I will complete it accurately," <<he>> says. "Do not expect either praise or punishment to appear where the questions do not ask for them."
			<br><br>
			The form stays on <<his>> desk for a week before <<he>> finally sends it.
		<</if>>
<</switch>>
<<run autismAssessmentAddSource("teacher")>>
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Hallways]]>><<unset $autismAssessmentTeacher>><<endevent>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Assessment Guardian
<<set $outside to 0>><<set $location to "home">><<effects>>
<<npc Bailey>><<person1>>
You find Bailey and hold out the assessment form.
<br><br>
<<He>> does not take it immediately. <<He>> leans forward, reads the heading, then looks at you.
<br><br>
"They want me to tell them what you were like when you were little?" <<he>> asks. "I've had a lot of kids through this place. You're going to get what I remember, not a bloody childhood biography."
<br><br>
<<if C.npc.Bailey.love gte 30 or $baileydefeated gte 3>>
	<<He>> finally takes the form. "I'll answer the parts I actually know. I'm not making things up just because the hospital left a blank box."
	<br><br>
	Three days later, <<he>> gives it back with about half of the questions completed and several others crossed out.
	<<run autismAssessmentAddSource("guardian")>>
<<else>>
	Bailey pushes the form back towards you. "No. I've got a house to run. I'm not volunteering for the hospital's paperwork too."
	<br><br>
	<<He>> starts walking away, then turns <<his>> head. "If somebody from the council wants to know whether you've always been like this, tell them yes. They can put that in whatever box they like."
	<<set $autismSpectrum.guardianRefused to true>><<stress 6>><<gstress>>
<</if>>
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Orphanage]]>><<endevent>><</link>>
<br>

:: Doctor Harper Autism Appointment
<<set $outside to 0>><<set $location to "hospital">><<effects>>
Harper enters your name into the appointment list, checks the available slot, and writes the date on a card before <<he>> hands it to you.
<br><br>
"Friday," <<he>> says. "Come to the hospital foyer and follow the sign to my office. Keep this card with the rest of the assessment paperwork so you do not have to remember the details from this conversation."
<br><br>
<<if autismHasDocumentation()>>
	<<He>> waits while you put the card with the report you already carry.
<<else>>
	<<He>> points to the card before letting go of it. "This is only an appointment reminder. You will not have a report until the assessment is complete."
<</if>>
<br><br>
<<if Time.weekDay is 6 and $daily.harperVisit isnot 1>>
	Harper glances at the clock. "Since it is already Friday, I have time to begin today's check-up now if you would rather not make another trip."
	<br><br>
	<<link [[Begin today's check-up|Doctor Harper's Office]]>><<set $daily.harperVisit to 1>><<endevent>><</link>>
	<br>
<</if>>
<<link [[Return to the foyer|Hospital Foyer]]>><<endevent>><</link>>
<br>

:: Doctor Harper Autism Appointment Later
<<endevent>>
<<set $outside to 0>><<set $location to "hospital">><<effects>>
<<harper_intro>>
You tell Harper that you have changed your mind and want the weekly appointment after all.
<br><br>
<<He>> opens the appointment list. "You said you might."
<br><br>
<<link [[Book it|Doctor Harper Autism Appointment]]>><<set $harper_appointments.enabled to true>><<unset $schoolPsych>><</link>>
<br>
<<link [[Change your mind again|Doctor Harper Autism Decline]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Doctor Harper Autism Decline
<<set $outside to 0>><<set $location to "hospital">><<effects>>
You tell Harper that you do not want a weekly appointment. <<He>> closes the appointment form without asking you to explain.
<br><br>
"Ask again whenever you want. The offer stays open."
<<if autismHasDocumentation()>>
	<<He>> nods towards the report. "Turning down the weekly appointment does not change your assessment or the report."
<<elseif autismAssessmentStage()>>
	"The assessment continues as planned," <<he>> says. "The weekly appointments are separate."
<<else>>
	<<He>> shrugs. "Come back if you change your mind."
<</if>>
<br><br>
<<link [[Return to the foyer|Hospital Foyer]]>><<endevent>><</link>>
<br>

:: Kylar Autism Documentation
<<effects>><<set $autismSpectrum.kylarDiscussed to true>><<run autismDiscloseTo("Kylar")>>
You take Harper's wallet out of your bag and tell Kylar where it came from. <<He>> reaches towards it, stops, wipes both palms on <<his>> clothes, then holds <<his>> hands out again.
<br><br>
"Can I read it? I won't fold anything or get marks on it."
<br><br>
You hand it over. Kylar sits down and opens the report across <<his>> knees. <<He>> reads the heading, turns to the second page, then goes back and reads the heading again.
<br><br>
"Harper wrote all of this about you," <<he>> says. <<He>> closes the report carefully but keeps both hands around it. "I have Autism too. There are reports at home. M-My parents kept every one, even the old ones they were supposed to throw away."
<br><br>
<<link [[Tell Kylar about Harper's questions|Kylar Autism Documentation Harper]]>><</link>>
<br>
<<link [[Ask how Autism affects <<him>>|Kylar Autism Documentation Kylar]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Kylar Autism Documentation Harper
<<effects>>
You tell Kylar about Harper's questions: noise, routines, and spending a long time focused on one subject. Kylar follows the page with one finger as you speak, finding each section in the report.
<br><br>
"Yeah. The bell hurts," <<he>> says. "I know when it's coming. That doesn't help.
I count the seconds until it rings. If anyone talks to me while I'm counting, I lose track and have to start
over."
<br><br>
You tell <<him>> which sounds do that to you. Kylar turns towards you quickly, and the report slips from <<his>> knees. You both reach for it at the same time, leaving <<his>> hand on top of yours. When footsteps approach, <<he>> pulls <<his>> hand away and smooths the paper flat.
<br><br>
<<include "Kylar Autism Documentation Offer">>

:: Kylar Autism Documentation Kylar
<<effects>>
Kylar opens <<his>> sketchbook to several drawings of the rear courtyard. The same tree stump appears from six different angles, with you somewhere in every drawing.
<br><br>
"Drawing is easier than talking, because I choose where everything goes." <<He>>
turns forward a few pages to a map of the school with times written next to the doors.
"I walk the same way every day. I know four other ways. If I change halfway through I lose track of what I was doing."
<br><br>
You point to a route between two classrooms you use. Kylar covers that line on the map with <<his>> palm.
<br><br>
"That one's only safe when you're with me," <<he>> says. <<He>> keeps <<his>> hand over the route until you look back at the report.
<br><br>
You ask whether that one is on the report too.
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Kylar Autism Documentation Kylar 1]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Kylar Autism Documentation Kylar 1
<<effects>>
Kylar moves <<his>> hand away from the map and looks down at the route.
<br><br>
"No, that's different," <<he>> says quickly. "The walking is part of it. The counting is. The bell hurting my ears is. Harper could write those things about me and use nearly the same words <<he>> used about you."
<br><br>
<<He>> places one finger beside the route <<he>> drew.
<br><br>
"But this isn't from a report. I know where you go because I kept watching you until I worked it out." <<His>> voice becomes quieter. "Nobody told me to follow you. I chose to do that. If you're angry about that, you can be angry about that separately. I won't say Autism made me do it."
<br><br>
<<He>> goes quiet and keeps <<his>> finger beside the route while you read.
<br><br>
<<include "Kylar Autism Documentation Offer">>

:: Kylar Autism Documentation Offer
Kylar finds the line about your current focused interest and reads it twice.
"<span class="gold"><<= autismKylarTrainingName()>></span>," <<he>> says. "That's what you've been spending so much time on this week."
<br><br>
You explain what you have been working on. Kylar places the report inside <<his>> sketchbook so the pages do not bend, then stands up holding both together.
<br><br>
"Take me with you," <<he>> says. "You can show me how it's done. I'll keep everyone else off you while you do it."
<br><br>
The report never suggested keeping other people away from you. Kylar added that part <<him>>self, and now waits for your answer.
<br><br>
<<link [[Train together|Kylar Autism Training Choice]]>><<npcincr Kylar love 1>><<npcincr Kylar rage -1>><</link>><<glove>><<lksuspicion>>
<br>
<<link [[Decline|Kylar Autism Training Decline]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Kylar Autism Training Return
<<effects>>
You tell Kylar you want to work on <span class="gold"><<= autismKylarTrainingName()>></span> today. <<He>> closes <<his>> sketchbook, pushes the pencils firmly into their holder, then stands.
<br><br>
"I remember last time," <<he>> says. "Tell me where we're going before we leave. I don't want the plan to change halfway there."
<br><br>
<<link [[Choose a place|Kylar Autism Training Choice]]>><</link>>
<br>
<<link [[Change your mind|Kylar Autism Training Decline]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Kylar Autism Training Decline
<<effects>>
You tell Kylar you do not want to train after all. <<He>> grips the edge of <<his>> sketchbook, then looks down and opens it to two blank pages.
<br><br>
"We could draw instead," <<he>> says, placing a pencil on the left page for you. "That page is yours. I won't draw on it unless you ask me to."
<br><br>
<<link [[Draw your focused interest together (0:30)|Kylar Autism Draw Fixation]]>><<pass 30>><<npcincr Kylar love 1>><<npcincr Kylar rage -1>><<stress -7>><</link>><<glove>><<lksuspicion>><<llstress>>
<br>
<<link [[Draw each other (0:30)|Kylar Autism Draw Portrait]]>><<pass 30>><<npcincr Kylar love 2>><<npcincr Kylar rage -3>><</link>><<gglove>><<llksuspicion>>
<br>
<<link [[Decline|Kylar Autism Return]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Kylar Autism Draw Fixation
<<effects>><<run autismFinishKylarTraining()>>
You start with the part of <span class="gold"><<= autismKylarTrainingName()>></span> you keep coming back to.
Kylar watches your pencil for a while, then starts the same thing on <<his>> page from another angle.
<br><br>
Every time you add something <<he>> asks why it goes there, and your answer gives <<him>> the next thing to draw.
<br><br>
You erase one line and redraw it further left. Kylar makes the same correction on <<his>> page and adds a small arrow beside it. <<He>> repeats this whenever you change something, so <<his>> page records each correction you made.
<br><br>
"Keep both," <<he>> says as <<he>> carefully tears <<his>> page out. "Mine shows which parts you had to change."
<br><br>
<<link [[Put the drawings away|Kylar Autism Return]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Kylar Autism Draw Portrait
<<effects>><<run autismFinishKylarTraining()>>
You sit facing Kylar and start drawing.
<br><br>
<<He>> holds still while you look at <<him>>, then shuffles forward a few inches every time you look down at your page.
The fourth time, you tell <<him>> to go back. <<He>> measures the gap you point at and moves back exactly that far.
<br><br>
Kylar finishes before you. <<He>> has drawn your clothes, hair, and face in careful detail. Your drawing of <<him>> is still missing one hand because <<he>> keeps hiding it under the sketchbook.
<br><br>
"Draw it holding yours," <<he>> says. <<He>> places the missing hand on the table with the palm facing up. You finish the drawing that way. When you are done, Kylar looks at the picture before curling <<his>> fingers closed.
<br><br>
<<link [[Put the drawings away|Kylar Autism Return]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Kylar Autism Training Choice
<<effects>><<set _focus to autismKylarTrainingFocus()>>
You list the places where you could work on <span class="gold"><<= autismKylarTrainingName()>></span>.
Kylar repeats them back in the order you said them, working out the walk to each one before <<he>> answers.
<br><br>
<<if ["physique", "athletics", "brawling", "footwork", "striking", "kicking", "willpower"].includes(_focus)>>
	<<if Time.hour gte 8 and Time.hour lt 21>>
		<<mmagymicon>><<link [[Go to the gym together (0:20)|Kylar Autism Gym]]>><<pass 20>><</link>>
	<<else>>
		<<mmagymicon>><span class="blue">The gym is shut at this hour.</span>
	<</if>>
	<br>
<</if>>
<<if ["swimming", "athletics", "willpower"].includes(_focus)>>
	<<beachicon>><<link [[Go to the beach together (0:25)|Kylar Autism Beach]]>><<pass 25>><</link>>
	<br>
<</if>>
<<if ["athletics", "dancing", "tending", "willpower"].includes(_focus)>>
	<<parkicon>><<link [[Go to the park together (0:15)|Kylar Autism Park]]>><<pass 15>><</link>>
	<br>
<</if>>
<<if ["science", "maths", "english", "history", "housekeeping"].includes(_focus)>>
	<<homeicon>><<link [[Go to the orphanage together (0:15)|Kylar Autism Orphanage]]>><<pass 15>><</link>>
	<br>
<</if>>
<<link [[Stay here|Kylar Autism Training Decline]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Kylar Autism Gym
<<set $outside to 0>><<set $location to "mma_gym">><<effects>>
Kylar enters the gym one step behind you and accidentally catches the back of your shoe. A heavy weight hits the floor in the training area beyond reception. Kylar immediately grabs your sleeve.
"What was that? Where did it come from?"
<br><br>
You point to the doorway leading into the training area. Kylar watches it for a few seconds, then releases your sleeve. <<He>> stays close to the wall while crossing the gym and only moves away from it when you reach the weight racks.
"Which one am I meant to take?"
<br><br>
You pick up a light dumbbell and show <<him>> how to hold it. While you demonstrate, Kylar grips the heavier one beside it with both hands and tries to lift it. <<His>> forearms begin to shake, but the weight barely moves. <<He>> bends down to check whether anything is holding it to the rack.
<br><br>
"That one's too heavy for you," you say.
<br><br>
Kylar lets go and wipes both palms on <<his>> clothes. "I knew that. I wanted to see whether you did."
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Kylar Autism Gym 2]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Kylar Autism Gym 2
<<effects>>
<<set _focus to autismKylarTrainingFocus()>>
<<if ["brawling", "footwork", "striking", "kicking", "willpower"].includes(_focus)>>
	You take <<him>> to the mats and show <<him>> where to place <<his>> feet. Kylar checks <<his>> position against yours several times before looking back up.
<<elseif _focus is "athletics">>
	You take the two treadmills at the end of the row. Kylar reads the panel on <<his>> twice,
	then waits with one hand on the rail for you to start yours first.
<<else>>
	You place two manageable weights on the floor with enough space to use them safely. Kylar waits beside them until you tell <<him>> what to do first.
<</if>>
<br><br>
<<link [[Start the session (1:00)|Kylar Autism Gym Training]]>><<pass 60>><</link>>
<br>
<<link [[Leave before training|Kylar Autism Leave]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Kylar Autism Gym Training
<<set $outside to 0>><<set $location to "mma_gym">><<effects>>
<<set _focus to autismKylarTrainingFocus()>>
<<if _focus is "physique">>
	Kylar chooses the heavier weight. Halfway through the first set, <<his>> wrists begin bending into a bad position, so you stop <<him>> and hand over the lighter one. <<He>> argues that <<he>> can manage the heavier weight, but takes the lighter one and copies the grip you showed <<him>>.
	<br><br>
	You work through your own sets between <<his>>, counting out loud so <<he>> has something to follow.
	<br><br>
	By the last set, <<his>> arms are shaking. You count more slowly so <<he>> has time to lower the weight safely instead of dropping it.
<<elseif _focus is "athletics">>
	You start your belt at a pace you can hold. Kylar puts <<his>> straight up to match it,
	loses the rhythm inside twenty seconds and grabs the rails.
	<br><br>
	You slow both machines down and get <<him>> to take shorter steps.
	<br><br>
	For the next few minutes, <<he>> watches your feet and matches your shorter steps. Once <<his>> pace becomes steady, you slowly raise the speed again in small steps.
<<else>>
	Kylar holds the stance while you fetch a pad from the rack and hold it up at the height you want
	<<him>> to hit.
	<br><br>
	The pad gives <<him>> a clear target. <<He>> checks its position before every strike, leaving a long pause between each hit.
	<br><br>
	You tell <<him>> to keep going until you drop the pad. <<He>> works through the whole sequence
	without looking up once, and the last hit moves your arm back further than the first did.
<</if>>
<<include "Kylar Autism Training Reward">>
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Kylar Autism Gym Training 2]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Kylar Autism Gym Training 2
<<effects>>
Kylar sits on the bench and drinks half a bottle of water before lowering it.
<br><br>
"Are you coming home after this?" <<he>> asks, once <<he>> has the breath for it.
"I can't leave you in here. Somebody else will offer to show you how to do it."
<br><br>
You gather your things. Kylar returns the light dumbbell to the rack, straightens it beside the heavier one, then walks out beside you.
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Kylar Autism Return]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Kylar Autism Beach
<<set $outside to 1>><<set $location to "beach">><<effects>>
Kylar stops where the path meets the sand and looks across the open beach. <<He>> remains there until you say <<his>> name.
<br><br>
"How many people are in the water?" <<he>> asks, then counts them <<himself>> before you can answer.
<br><br>
<<He>> carries the bag farther from the people near the water and puts it where <<he>> can see both the sea and the path back to the road.
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Kylar Autism Beach 2]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Kylar Autism Beach 2
<<effects>>
<<set _focus to autismKylarTrainingFocus()>>
<<if _focus is "swimming">>
	You walk down into the water. Kylar follows until it comes over <<his>> knees, then takes hold of your wrist.
	<br><br>
	"Tell me before you go under," <<he>> says. You show <<him>> the stretch you mean to swim and the line back to the bag,
	and <<he>> makes you point at both of them twice before <<he>> lets go.
<<elseif _focus is "athletics">>
	You explain that running on wet sand takes more effort, so it is useful for training.
	<br><br>
	Kylar presses one shoe into the wet sand and watches the mark change as water enters it. <<He>> draws a starting line with <<his>> heel, walks twenty paces, then draws another.
<<else>>
	You tell <<him>> you want to stay in the cold water instead of coming straight back out of it.
	Kylar puts two fingers in, takes them out, dries them on <<his>> sleeve, and asks how long each go
	is meant to last.
	<br><br>
	<<He>> wants the number before you start, so you give <<him>> one.
<</if>>
<br><br>
<<link [[Begin practising (1:00)|Kylar Autism Beach Training]]>><<pass 60>><</link>>
<br>
<<link [[Leave before training|Kylar Autism Leave]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Kylar Autism Beach Training
<<set $outside to 1>><<set $location to "beach">><<effects>>
<<set _focus to autismKylarTrainingFocus()>>
<<if _focus is "swimming">>
	You start inside the stretch you pointed out to <<him>>, close to shore. Kylar lifts <<his>> head
	out of the water after every second stroke to find you, so you swim beside <<him>> instead of ahead,
	and that stops.
	<br><br>
	You show <<him>> how to turn without putting <<his>> feet down. <<He>> takes a mouthful of water on
	the first try and coughs it out against your shoulder, then goes straight back and does it again.
	<br><br>
	The second turn is still slow, but <<he>> completes it without putting <<his>> feet down.
<<elseif _focus is "athletics">>
	You run between two lines Kylar drew in the sand.
	<br><br>
	<<He>> goes off far too fast on the first length
	and has nothing left by the turn, so you shorten the course and set a pace <<he>> can actually hold.
	After four or five lengths you stretch it out again.
	<br><br>
	By the end, <<he>> turns at the same point you do and keeps a steadier pace beside you.
<<else>>
	You walk in together on the count you gave <<him>>, saying the numbers out loud.
	<br><br>
	Kylar starts trying to leave when the water reaches <<his>> waist, so you keep
	counting at the same speed and don't move. <<He>> stays. On the next go <<he>> gets to the end
	of the count without pulling at your arm, and comes out when you do instead of before you.
<</if>>
<<include "Kylar Autism Training Reward">>
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Kylar Autism Beach Training 2]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Kylar Autism Beach Training 2
<<effects>>
You collect your things. Kylar notices sand on the back of your clothes and brushes it away, but keeps rubbing the same spot after the sand is gone. You move <<his>> hand aside.
<br><br>
On the way up the path <<he>> takes the side nearest the road and looks back whenever you drop more than a step behind.
<br><br>
<<link [[Walk back with Kylar|Kylar Autism Return]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Kylar Autism Park
<<set $outside to 1>><<set $location to "park">><<effects>>
<<set _focus to autismKylarTrainingFocus()>>
You find an open patch of grass away from the main path. Kylar walks around its edge before choosing a place to sit with the hedge behind <<him>>.
<br><br>
A jogger passes on the other side of the hedge. Kylar turns towards the sound but cannot see anyone through the leaves. The same thing happens twice more, and each time <<he>> stops speaking to check behind <<him>>.
<br><br>
"I can't hear which ones are coming closer," <<he>> says. You move so you are facing the path and
<<hes>> facing you, and tell <<him>> you'll say something if anyone stops. After that <<he>> only turns once.
<br><br>
<<switch _focus>>
	<<case "athletics">>You walk the training route together first so Kylar knows where it goes. <<He>> points out two roots sticking out of the ground and steps on the second one to show you exactly where it is.
	<<case "willpower">>For this practice, you move to a bench beside the busy path. You explain that the goal is to keep working while people pass nearby. Kylar writes the task and finish time at the top of a page, then turns it so both of you can see it.
	<<case "dancing">>You show <<him>> the steps at half speed. Kylar counts quietly and stops whenever your foot lands somewhere different from where <<he>> expected.
	<<case "tending">>You move to a nearby flower bed and kneel beside the plants. Kylar sits between you and the path, then compares the leaves carefully before letting you pull out the first weed.
<</switch>>
<br><br>
<<link [[Begin practising (1:00)|Kylar Autism Park Training]]>><<pass 60>><</link>>
<br>
<<link [[Leave before training|Kylar Autism Leave]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Kylar Autism Park Training
<<set $outside to 1>><<set $location to "park">><<effects>>
<<set _focus to autismKylarTrainingFocus()>>
<<switch _focus>>
	<<case "athletics">>You run the route together. Kylar calls out both roots on the first lap, then a bench, then a bin, and by the third lap <<hes>> too short of breath to finish. You ask <<him>> to save it for anything that has actually changed. <<He>> says nothing at all for the next two laps, which is its own problem, and then starts calling the wet patches.<br><br>By the last lap <<hes>> running level with you instead of talking over <<his>> shoulder.
	<<case "willpower">>You work down the page while people walk close behind the bench and a group of children run the length of the path twice. Kylar loses <<his>> line every time somebody slows down near you. You tap the word <<he>> stopped at so <<he>> can pick it up without going back to the top, and after the third time <<he>> starts doing the same for you when your head comes up.
	<<case "dancing">>You go through the short routine until Kylar stops watching only your feet. <<He>> misses the turn when your shoulder brushes <<his>>, steps back into place and picks the count up where it was. The next three turns hold.
	<<case "tending">>You break up the soil while Kylar sorts what comes out. <<He>> asks you to check the first few, then stops asking and starts showing you each root before <<he>> puts the weed on the pile. The bed has straight rows in it by the time you stand up.
<</switch>>
<<include "Kylar Autism Training Reward">>
<br><br>
Kylar walks the edge of the grass once more before <<he>> follows you onto the path,
and takes the side nearest the road without saying anything about it.
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Kylar Autism Return]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Kylar Autism Orphanage
<<set $outside to 0>><<set $location to "home">><<effects>>
<<set _focus to autismKylarTrainingFocus()>>
Kylar stops inside the door. "Is Bailey in?" <<he>> asks, and doesn't move until you answer.
<br><br>
You tell <<him>> where Bailey will be, and that nobody comes through this room on the way to anywhere.
Kylar looks towards the doorway again after you explain. Then <<he>> follows you to a table and chooses the chair facing the door,
leaving you the one against the wall.
<br><br>
Two orphans come over before you have anything on the table, and a third leans on the back of Kylar's
chair to see what <<hes>> holding. Kylar folds both arms over the sketchbook and stops speaking.
<br><br>
You hand the nearest one a spare pencil and tell them the table is taken for an hour. They go,
and the others follow. Kylar watches the door they leave by for a while afterwards.
<br><br>
<<if ["science", "maths", "english", "history"].includes(_focus)>>
	You put the book down between you and open it. Kylar reads the contents page, finds the section you want,
	and folds the corner of the page before it so <<he>> can get back there fast.
<<else>>
	You bring over a basket of clean laundry. Kylar starts sorting it by owner, by colour,
	then gets stuck on a shirt that could go on either of two piles. You turn the collar over and show
	<<him>> the name sewn into it.
<</if>>
<br><br>
<<link [[Work together (1:00)|Kylar Autism Orphanage Training]]>><<pass 60>><</link>>
<br>
<<link [[Stop before starting|Kylar Autism Leave]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Kylar Autism Orphanage Training
<<set $outside to 0>><<set $location to "home">><<effects>>
<<set _focus to autismKylarTrainingFocus()>>
<<switch _focus>>
	<<case "science">>You copy the diagram out of the book, then cover the book and draw it again from memory. Kylar spots that you have two labels the wrong way round before you do. <<He>> finds the line of text that proves it, waits while you fix the page, then puts <<his>> hand over the book for you to try a third time.
	<<case "maths">>You work the same problem two ways. Kylar writes out every line even after <<he>> has the answer, so when your results disagree you can walk back up <<his>> page together. You find it eleven lines down, where a seven became a one.
	<<case "english">>You read the passage aloud and discuss why the two characters speak differently. Kylar underlines repeated words, then reads the next paragraph while skipping them. The sentence sounds different without the repetition, making it easier to hear which words were being emphasised.
	<<case "history">>You lay the dates out across the table and join each event to the one it caused. Kylar moves one of your cards two places along. You make <<him>> justify it, so <<he>> goes back to the book, finds the paragraph and reads it to you with <<his>> finger under the line.
	<<case "housekeeping">>You fold while Kylar checks the collars. <<He>> starts unfolding your work to redo any fold that isn't square, which stops the pile growing at all, so you give <<him>> the socks and the small things and take the rest yourself. The basket empties without either of you starting a pile over.
<</switch>>
<<include "Kylar Autism Training Reward">>
<br><br>
One of the orphans comes back and asks whether Kylar will visit again. <<He>> looks at you before answering.
"When <<pshe "Kylar">> brings me," <<he>> says. <<He>> puts away the things you used, straightens the table, then waits by the door while you collect your belongings.
<br><br>
<<link [[Walk out together|Kylar Autism Return]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Kylar Autism Training Reward
<<set _autismKylarTraining to true>>
<<set _focus to autismKylarTrainingFocus()>>
<<switch _focus>>
	<<case "science">><<scienceskill 5>><<ggscience>>
	<<case "maths">><<mathsskill 5>><<ggmaths>>
	<<case "english">><<englishskill 5>><<ggenglish>>
	<<case "history">><<historyskill 5>><<gghistory>>
	<<case "physique">><<physique 5>><<ggphysique>>
	<<case "athletics">><<athletics 5>><<ggathletics>>
	<<case "willpower">><<willpower 5>><<ggwillpower>>
	<<case "swimming">><<swimmingskilluse>><<ggswimming>>
	<<case "dancing">><<danceskill 5>><<ggdanceskill>>
	<<case "tending">><<tending 5>><<ggtending>>
	<<case "housekeeping">><<housekeeping 5>><<gghousekeeping>>
	<<case "brawling">>
		<<set _gain to 5 * autismStatGainMultiplier("brawling", 5, "training") * (($statGainMult / 100) * (1 + 0.5 * $featsBoosts.upgrades.transcend / 100))>>
		<<if $combatExtended.brawling gt 1000 or $AMCTraits.halfGain is 1>><<set _gain /= $AMCTraits.brawling>><</if>>
		<<set $combatExtended.brawling to amcClamp($combatExtended.brawling + _gain, 1000, $AMCTraits.brawling, $combatExtended.brawling)>><span class="green">Your brawling skill improves.</span>
	<<case "footwork">><<footwork 5>><<ggfootwork>>
	<<case "striking">><<striking 5>><<ggstriking>>
	<<case "kicking">><<kicking 5>><<ggkicking>>
<</switch>>
<<stress -6>><<lstress>><<npcincr Kylar love 1>><<glove>>
<<run autismFinishKylarTraining()>>
<br><br>

:: Kylar Autism Leave
<<effects>>
<<run delete $autismSpectrum.kylarEventFocus>>
You tell Kylar you're not going to start after all. <<He>> looks at the spot where you were going to work,
then puts <<his>> sketchbook away.
<br><br>
<<switch $location>>
	<<case "mma_gym">><<He>> returns the light dumbbell to the rack, then walks around the people using the mats rather than passing close to them.
	<<case "beach">><<He>> knocks the sand out of both shoes against the path before <<he>> puts them back on.
	<<case "park">><<He>> walks the edge of the grass once, then follows you to the gate.
	<<case "home">><<He>> puts the supplies back where you got them and checks the table twice on the way past.
<</switch>>
<br><br>
Kylar walks beside you on the way out. "Tell me before we leave next time if you might change your mind," <<he>> says. "I came here expecting to do this."
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Kylar Autism Return]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Kylar Autism Return
<<if $autismSpectrum>><<run delete $autismSpectrum.kylarEventFocus>><</if>>
<<if $autismSpectrum and $autismSpectrum.kylarReturn>>
	<<set $location to $autismSpectrum.kylarReturn.location>>
	<<set $outside to $autismSpectrum.kylarReturn.outside>>
	<<run delete $autismSpectrum.kylarReturn>>
<</if>>
<<effects>>
<<kylaroptions "notext">>

/* OVERLOAD */
:: Autism Hallway Overload
<<set $weekly.autismAwareness to true>>
<<run autismSocialDemand(2)>><<generatey1>><<person1>>
The lunch bell rings, and students begin filling the corridor.
Lockers open and close around you while people talk over each other on their way past.
<br><br>
A student comes up beside you. "You're coming, right?"
<br><br>
You turn towards <<him>>, but before you can ask where <<he>> means, someone behind <<him>> asks you about the homework.
Another student starts telling a joke at the same time.
You try to follow all three conversations, but each new voice interrupts the last.
<br><br>
By the time you understand one question, someone is already waiting for an answer to another.
<br><br>
<<link [[Ask them to say one thing at a time|Autism Hallway Overload Direct]]>><</link>>
<br>
<<link [[Step into an empty classroom (0:10)|Autism Hallway Overload Quiet]]>><<pass 10>><</link>>
<br>
<<link [[Pretend you understood and follow them (0:05)|Autism Hallway Overload Mask]]>><<pass 5>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Hallway Overload Direct
<<set $outside to 0>><<set $location to "school">><<effects>>
<<run autismRestBreak(2)>>
"One thing at a time," you say. "Which question do you want me to answer?"
<br><br>
The students stop talking over each other. One of them looks embarrassed, while the first student nods and asks,
"Are you coming to lunch?"
<br><br>
Now that only one person is speaking, you can answer without struggling to follow several questions at once.
<<stress -2>><<lstress>>
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Hallways]]>><<endevent>><<set $eventskip to 1>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Hallway Overload Quiet
<<set $outside to 0>><<set $location to "school">><<effects>>
<<run autismRestBreak(3)>>
You raise a hand to excuse yourself and step into the nearest empty classroom. After closing the door, most of the corridor noise is blocked by the wall.
<br><br>
You sit alone and focus on your breathing while the noise outside continues at a lower volume. After several minutes, you can separate one sound from another again and think through what you were doing.
<<stress -3>><<lstress>>
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Hallways]]>><<endevent>><<set $eventskip to 1>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Hallway Overload Mask
<<set $outside to 0>><<set $location to "school">><<effects>>
<<run autismSocialDemand(2)>><<person1>>
You smile and nod while the others talk, laughing when one of the students laughs.
<br><br>
Most of them keep talking. The first student keeps looking at your face. "Are you sure?" <<he>> asks.
You nod again, and <<he>> lets it go. <<He>> noticed that something was wrong, even if the others did not.
<br><br>
When the group starts walking towards the canteen, you go with them.
<br><br>
As you follow, you try to remember what they were talking about.
You still do not know what you just agreed to.
<br><br>
Pretending you understood helped you avoid an awkward question,
but keeping up the act left you <span class="red">more tired and stressed</span>.
<<stress 2>><<gstress>><<tiredness 1>><<gtiredness>>
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Canteen]]>><<endevent>><<set $eventskip to 1>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Overload
<<effects>>
<<set _form to autismOverloadForm()>><<set _place to autismOverloadPlace()>>
/* A shutdown always goes through Autism Shutdown, which chooses how much of the opening to describe.
   The shutdown and meltdown option lists are not interchangeable: nothing below this point is offered
   to a PC who cannot move or speak. */
<<if _form is "shutdown">>
	<<include "Autism Shutdown">>
<<else>>
	The sounds around you begin to overlap until you can no longer focus on one at a time.
	There is too much happening around you to think clearly.
	<br><br>
	Your hands begin repeating the same movement while your breathing becomes short and fast. You make a sound without meaning to, then keep making it even after you notice.
	<<if random(1, 3) is 1>>
		<br><br>
		You keep repeating the same word because it takes less effort than trying to form a full sentence.
	<</if>>
	<br><br>
	<<switch _place>>
		<<case "alone">>
			There is nobody nearby, so at least you do not have to deal with anyone watching or asking questions.
		<<case "known">>
			<<generate1>><<person1>>
			Someone comes to the doorway and stops when <<he>> sees you. "What's wrong?" <<he>> asks,
			then repeats the question when you do not answer.
			<br><br>
			Each repeated question gives you another thing to process. Someone farther away tells <<him>> to give you a minute, so <<he>> stays by the doorway instead of coming closer. You keep wishing everyone would stop talking until you can recover.
		<<default>>
			<<generate1>><<generate2>><<person1>>
			People begin to slow down as they pass, and one person stops to watch you.
			<br><br>
			A <<person>> says you must be on drugs. <<person2>>A <<person>> tells <<his>> friend that you are pretending.
			Someone else raises a phone and points it at you.
			<br><br>
			<<person1>>Before you can react, the <<person>> grabs your arm and tries to pull you to your feet.
			"Alright, that's enough," <<he>> says.
			<br><br>
			The sudden touch adds another sensation while you are already struggling with the voices and people around you. You pull against <<his>> grip, unable to focus on anything else.
			<<run autismSocialDemand(6)>><<stress 8>><<ggstress>>
	<</switch>>
	<br><br>
	<<link [[Stim (0:15)|Autism Overload Stim]]>><<pass 15>><</link>>
	<br>
	<<if _place isnot "alone">>
		<<if autismHasDocumentation()>>
			<<link [[Hold out Harper's card instead of speaking (0:05)|Autism Overload Card]]>><<pass 5>><</link>>
			<br>
		<</if>>
		<<link [[Get away from them (0:10)|Autism Overload Leave]]>><<pass 10>><</link>>
		<br>
	<</if>>
	<<link [[Hide how overwhelmed you are (0:05)|Autism Overload Mask]]>><<pass 5>><</link>>
	<br>
<</if>>

:: Autism Overload Stim
<<effects>>
<<set _loadBefore to ensureAutismSpectrumState().load>>
<<set _place to autismStim(15, autismOverloadPlace())>>
<<set _loadDrop to _loadBefore - ensureAutismSpectrumState().load>>
You repeat the same movement with your hands at a steady pace. Watching and feeling the movement gives you one simple thing to focus on while the other sounds and sensations continue around you.<<stress -3>><<lstress>>
<br><br>
<<switch _place>>
	<<case "alone">>
		With nobody nearby, you do not have to hide what you are doing.
		You rock back and forth, move your hands as much as you need, and let yourself make noise.
		<br><br>
		After a while, you can separate the sounds around you more easily. One voice or noise can hold your attention without every other sound interrupting it.
		<<autism-stim-result _loadDrop>><<tiredness -3>><<lltiredness>><<stress -2>><<lstress>>
	<<case "known">>
		You keep the movement mostly in your hands so nobody pays much attention.
		Your fingers repeat the same pattern while you concentrate on it.
		<br><br>
		The hand movement helps, though you keep stopping yourself from rocking or making sounds because other people are nearby.
		<<autism-stim-result _loadDrop>><<tiredness -2>><<ltiredness>><<stress -2>><<lstress>>
	<<default>>
		You keep the movement small enough to hide. Your thumb presses against each fingertip beneath your sleeve,
		repeating the same pattern while you try to keep anyone from noticing.
		<br><br>
		Hiding it takes some effort, but the repeated movement still helps.
		<<autism-stim-result _loadDrop>><<tiredness -1>><<ltiredness>><<stress -2>><<lstress>>
<</switch>>
<br><br>
<<if autismInMeltdown()>>
	You are still overwhelmed, but you can follow your thoughts more easily than before. Repeating the movement longer may help further.
	<br><br>
	<<link [[Keep going (0:15)|Autism Overload Stim]]>><<pass 15>><</link>>
	<br>
<</if>>
<<link [[Move on|Autism Overload End]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Shutdown
<<set _place to autismOverloadPlace()>>
<<if !$autismSpectrum.shutdownSeen>>
	<<set $autismSpectrum.shutdownSeen to true>>
	You try to speak, but your mouth does not move. You shorten the answer to one word and try again, but nothing comes out.
	<br><br>
	Your hands stop moving. You try to lift one, but it stays where it is. Even turning your head feels difficult.
	<br><br>
	You focus on a mark on the ground and keep looking at it. Concentrating on one small detail is easier than trying to respond to everything else around you.
<<else>>
	You recognise it before it finishes arriving. The words stop first, then your hands, and you are left looking at
	one fixed point because looking at it is easier than anything else available to you.
	<br><br>
	<<if random(1, 3) is 1>>
		You notice yourself repeating the last three words someone said to you. You have already said them
		several times without deciding to.
		<br><br>
	<</if>>
	Knowing what it is does not shorten it.
<</if>>
<br><br>
<<if _place is "alone">>
	<<link [[Nobody is here. Wait.|Autism Shutdown Alone]]>><<pass 10>><</link>>
	<br>
<<else>>
	Somebody is talking to you.
	<br><br>
	<<link [[Next|Autism Shutdown Asked]]>><</link>>
	<br>
<</if>>

:: Autism Shutdown Alone
<<effects>>
Nobody comes over or asks you questions.
<br><br>
You remain where you are and wait. After some time, forming words becomes easier. You quietly say one word, then another, to check that you can speak again.
<br><br>
You try to stand and fail on the first attempt. On the second, your legs respond and you get to your feet. You are still shaken, but your body is moving normally again.
<<stress -8>><<lstress>>
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Autism Overload End]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Shutdown Asked
<<effects>>
<<if $NPCList.length gt 0 and isLiveNPC($NPCList[0])>><<person1>><<else>><<generatey1>><<person1>><</if>>
"...Alright? I said are you alright?"
<br><br>
You understand every word of the question, but you cannot make yourself answer.
The <<person>> waits for a reply, watching your face while the silence continues.
<br><br>
You cannot speak yet, but you may still be able to make <span class="gold">a small movement</span>.
<br>
<<link [[Manage a single word|Autism Shutdown Word]]>><</link>>
<br>
<<if autismHasDocumentation()>>
	<<link [[Get the card out of your bag|Autism Overload Card]]>><<pass 5>><</link>>
	<br>
<</if>>
<<link [[Keep looking at the fixed point and wait|Autism Shutdown Wait]]>><<pass 10>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Shutdown Word
<<effects>><<person1>>
You force out one word: "fine". It is not what you wanted to say, but it is the only word you can manage.
<br><br>
<<if C.npc.Kylar and C.npc.Kylar.init is 1 and $autismSpectrum.sceneReturn and $autismSpectrum.sceneReturn.passage is "Hallways" and $location is "school" and random(1, 3) is 1>>
	Kylar appears nearby and immediately says, "<<pShes "Kylar">> not fine. Leave <<phim "Kylar">> alone." <<nnpc_He "Kylar">> steps between you and the <<person>>, who finally leaves.
	<<stress -6>><<lstress>>
<<else>>
	The <<person>> accepts the answer even though <<he>> looks uncertain, then leaves.
	<br><br>
	You are finally left alone, but forcing the word out has taken a lot of effort.
	<<stress -3>><<lstress>>
<</if>>
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Autism Overload End]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Shutdown Wait
<<effects>><<person1>>
You keep looking at the same point and do not try to answer the next question.
<br><br>
<<set _patient to autismHasPreference("space") or autismHasPreference("plain") or autismDisclosedTo("nurse")>>
<<if _patient and $location is "school">>
	The <<person>> starts to repeat the question, then stops.
	<br><br>
	"Okay," <<he>> says. "You don't have to answer. I'm going to stand over there." <<He>> moves several steps away and stops asking questions.
	<br><br>
	With nobody waiting for an answer, you can focus on getting your breathing and movement under control again.
	The person gives you space and <span class="green">stops adding more demands</span>.<<stress -8>><<lstress>>
<<else>>
	The <<person>> repeats the question more loudly. When you still do not answer, <<he>> grabs your arm to get your attention.
	<br><br>
	You were already listening. The sudden touch gives you another sensation to process and interrupts the small amount of control you were starting to regain.
	The shouting and unexpected touch <span class="red">make the shutdown worse</span>.<<stress 10>><<ggstress>><<trauma 4>><<gtrauma>>
<</if>>
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Autism Overload End]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Overload Card
<<effects>>
/* The card can be held out from either opening, and the shutdown route only ever has one onlooker. */
<<if $NPCList.length is 0 or !isLiveNPC($NPCList[0])>><<generatey1>><</if>>
You manage to open your wallet and pull out the card. Speaking still feels impossible,
so you hold it out where the people around you can read it.
<br><br>
<<if autismOverloadPlace() is "known">>
	<<person1>><<He>> reads the card without coming any closer. After a few seconds,
	<<he>> stops asking you questions and gives you more space.
	<br><br>
	<<He>> sits on the floor a short distance away and looks elsewhere. <<He>> stays nearby without speaking to you.
	You are given <span class="green">time and space to recover</span>.
	<<stress -6>><<llstress>>
<<else>>
	<<if $NPCList.length gte 2 and isLiveNPC($NPCList[1])>>
		<<person1>>The <<person>> holding your arm looks down at the card. <<He>> reads it, releases you, and steps back.
		Two of the others lean close enough to read it over <<his>> shoulder. Once they understand, they move away as well.
		<br><br>
		<<person2>>The <<person>> who called you a pretender stays nearby.
		<<He>> tells the others to leave you alone, then steps between you and the person holding the phone so
		they cannot keep crowding you.
		<br><br>
		The people around you move back, <span class="green">leaving you more room</span>.
	<<else>>
		<<person1>>The <<person>> takes the card out of your hand, reads it, and hands it back without saying anything else.
		<br><br>
		<<He>> steps back to give you room, then turns to the people slowing down behind <<him>> and tells them to keep moving.
		<br><br>
		<span class="green">Nobody crowds you while you recover.</span>
	<</if>>
	<<stress -4>><<lstress>>
<</if>>
<br><br>
You still cannot explain what you need aloud, but the written instructions tell the people around you what to do.
<br><br>
<<link [[Stim (0:15)|Autism Overload Stim]]>><<pass 15>><</link>>
<br>
<<link [[End this|Autism Overload End]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Overload Leave
<<effects>>
You force yourself to move away from the crowd. Your steps are slow and uneven,
but each one puts more distance between you and the noise.
<br><br>
Someone calls after you, but you keep walking.
<br><br>
As you move farther away, the voices become quieter. You can separate individual sounds again instead of hearing everything at the same intensity.
<<run autismRestBreak(6)>><<stress -4>><<lstress>>
<br><br>
<<link [[Stim (0:15)|Autism Overload Stim]]>><<pass 15>><</link>>
<br>
<<link [[End this|Autism Overload End]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Overload Mask
<<effects>>
You try to hide the overload. You lower your shoulders, hold your hands still, and control your breathing so people nearby will think you are calm.
<br><br>
<<if autismOverloadPlace() is "known">>
	<<person1>>Someone you know keeps an eye on you after the others look away.
	<<He>> stays nearby without crowding you, then speaks quietly enough that nobody else can hear.
	"You don't have to act like you're fine with me."
	<br><br>
	<<He>> does not push you to answer, but <<he>> clearly noticed that you are still struggling.
	<br><br>
<<else>>
	Once you seem all right again, the people around you lose interest and go back to what they were doing.
<</if>>

The noise and discomfort are still affecting you. On top of that, you have to keep checking your face, hands, and breathing so nobody notices.
<<run autismSocialDemand(10)>><<trauma 4>><<gtrauma>>
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Autism Overload End]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Overload End
<<set _autismState to ensureAutismSpectrumState()>>
<<if _autismState.sceneReturn>>
	<<set _return to _autismState.sceneReturn.passage>>
	<<set $location to _autismState.sceneReturn.location>>
	<<set $outside to _autismState.sceneReturn.outside>>
<<else>>
	<<set _return to "Bedroom">>
<</if>>
<<effects>>
<<if autismInMeltdown()>>
	You can move again, but the <<= autismOverloadForm()>> has not passed. Your body feels heavy and your
	thoughts are still difficult to organise, so even simple actions take more effort than usual.
<<else>>
	The worst of it has passed, though you still need time to recover.
	<br><br>
	You can separate the sounds around you again, and your muscles begin to relax. You are still exhausted and will need more than a short rest to recover fully.
<</if>>
<br><br>
<<link "Carry on" _return>>
	<<unset $autismSpectrum.sceneReturn>>
	<<unset $autismSpectrum.overloadPlace>>
	<<if !$autismSpectrum.meltdown>>
		<<unset $autismSpectrum.overloadForm>>
	<</if>>
	<<endevent phaseless>>
<</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Focus
<<set _autismState to ensureAutismSpectrumState()>>
<<set _back to _autismState.focusReturn || "Bedroom">>
<<effects>>
<<set _focus to autismHyperfixationName()>>
<<set _category to autismHyperfixationCategory()>>
<<switch _category>>
	<<case "academic">>
		You take out everything you have on <<= _focus>> and spend the next half hour reading and working without checking the time.
		<br><br>
		Nobody assigned this work. You continue because each answer gives you another question to check, and you want to keep going.
	<<case "physical">>
		You work through <<= _focus>> on your own, without counting, with nobody timing you.
		<br><br>
		You repeat the same movement several times, watching for small mistakes. Each time you notice one, you correct it on the next attempt.
	<<case "practical">>
		You give the next half hour to <<= _focus>>, and do it properly instead of quickly.
		<br><br>
		You decide where each item should go and work through them one by one. When you finish, the result is clear in front of you.
	<<case "combat">>
		You run through <<= _focus>> alone, slowly, then again, changing one small thing each time.
		<br><br>
		You can practise each movement slowly without anyone watching. After several attempts, the motion becomes smoother and more accurate.
	<<default>>
		You spend the next half hour thinking about <<= _focus>> because you want to. You follow one detail into another without needing to switch to something else.
		<br><br>
		By the end, you feel less mentally tired than when you started.
<</switch>>
<<run autismRestBreak(4)>><<stress -5>><<lstress>>
<br><br>
<<link "Next" _back>><<run delete $autismSpectrum.focusReturn>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Aftermath Hallway
<<set $outside to 0>><<set $location to "school">><<schooleffects>><<effects>>
<<set $autismSpectrum.aftermathDone to true>>
<<set _form to autismLastOverloadForm()>>
<<set _whitneyHere to C.npc.Whitney.init is 1 and $whitneyget isnot 1>>
<<if _whitneyHere and C.npc.Whitney.love lt 40>>
	<<npc Whitney>><<person1>>
	Whitney catches up with you between lessons and falls into step beside you without asking.
	<<if _form is "shutdown">>
		"Oi, slut. You went properly fucking weird the other day," <<he>> says. "Not your usual sulking either. People kept talking at you and you just stood there like none of them existed."
		<br><br>
		<<He>> watches you from the side. "Everyone thought you were being a prick on purpose. I told them you probably weren't."
		<br><br>
		You turn towards <<him>>. Whitney immediately looks ahead again. "Don't make a thing of it. They were getting boring."
	<<else>>
		"That was one hell of a show you put on," Whitney says. "Right in the middle of school as well. Couldn't have picked somewhere less public, could you?"
		<br><br>
		You do not answer. <<He>> looks at you, then nudges your arm with <<his>> elbow. "Relax, slut. Half the school had found something else to gossip about by dinner."
		<br><br>
		<<He>> waits another step before adding, "I remembered, obviously. Hard to forget you screaming at everyone."
	<</if>>
	<<stress 3>><<gstress>>
<<else>>
	<<generatey1>><<person1>>
	A <<person>> you recognise from school catches up with you between lessons. <<He>> hesitates before speaking.
	<<if _form is "shutdown">>
		"You alright after the other day? You stopped answering everyone and I couldn't tell if you could still hear us."
		<br><br>
		<<He>> rubs the back of <<his>> neck. "Someone said you'd fainted, but you were still standing up, so that didn't make sense either. I just wanted to check you were alright now."
	<<else>>
		"That looked horrible the other day," <<he>> says. "I kept thinking I should do something, but I had no idea what would actually help."
		<br><br>
		When you do not answer immediately, <<he>> gives you room instead of asking again. "Anyway, I hope you're doing better today."
	<</if>>
<</if>>
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Hallways]]>><<endevent>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Stim
<<set _autismState to ensureAutismSpectrumState()>>
<<set _back to _autismState.stimReturn || "Bedroom">>
<<effects>>
<<set _loadBefore to ensureAutismSpectrumState().load>>
<<set _place to autismStim(15, autismPrivacyTier(_back))>>
<<set _loadDrop to _loadBefore - ensureAutismSpectrumState().load>>
<<switch _place>>
	<<case "alone">>
		<<if _back is "Temple Bunk">>Behind the dressing screen, out of sight of the other initiates,<<else>>With nobody around,<</if>> you let your hands and feet move as much as you need. You repeat the familiar motions until your breathing slows and you can follow one thought at a time.
		<br><br>
		After fifteen minutes you are ready to get on with the day.
		<<autism-stim-result _loadDrop>><<tiredness -3>><<lltiredness>><<stress -2>><<lstress>>
	<<case "known">>
		You repeat the familiar movements with your hands and feet. The people here have seen you do this before,
		so nobody comments or pays much attention.
		<br><br>
		You move more carefully than when you're alone, but being with someone you know helps you relax.
		<<autism-stim-result _loadDrop>><<tiredness -2>><<ltiredness>><<stress -2>><<lstress>>
	<<default>>
		With people nearby you keep the movements small, tapping each finger against your thumb and turning one
		foot against the floor.
		<br><br>
		The repeated movement helps, but you keep checking its size so nobody notices. That extra effort reduces how much rest you get from it.
		<<autism-stim-result _loadDrop>><<tiredness -1>><<ltiredness>><<stress -2>><<lstress>>
<</switch>>
<br><br>
<<link "Next" _back>><<run delete $autismSpectrum.stimReturn>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Lesson
<<set $outside to 0>><<set $location to "school">><<schooleffects>><<effects>>
<<set _teacherKnows to autismTeacherKnows($autismLessonTeacher)>>
<<set _phase to autismSpectrumPhase()>>
<<switch $autismLessonTeacher>>
	<<case "Sirris">><<npc Sirris>>
	<<case "Doren">><<npc Doren>>
	<<case "Winter">><<npc Winter>>
	<<default>><<npc River>>
<</switch>><<person1>>
<<if _phase is "burnout">>
	The class has moved on to another part of the lesson. The board has changed twice since the last instruction you understood, and everyone else is already writing.
<<else>>
	Your teacher has changed tasks twice in five minutes, but you are still finishing the first one. You miss the next instruction while trying to complete what you were already doing.
<</if>>
<br><br>
<<switch $autismLessonTeacher>>
	<<case "Doren">>
		Doren notices your empty page from the front of the room. "Hey, are you still with me back there? I asked everyone to move on, but I can go over it again."
	<<case "River">>
		River stops speaking and looks towards your desk. "You at the back. I've asked twice. Are you following this or not?"
	<<case "Sirris">>
		Sirris looks directly at you. "Are you alright, dear? I've asked you twice and you haven't answered me." Several students turn to see who <<he>> means.
	<<default>>
		Winter stops beside the board and fixes you with a look. "I have repeated the instruction twice. Are you listening, or do I need to repeat it a third time?"
<</switch>>
<br><br>
<<if _teacherKnows>>
	You have <span class="gold">Doctor Harper's card</span> in your bag.
	<br>
<</if>>
<<icon "antiques/antique-watch.gif">><<link [[Ask <<him>> to say it one thing at a time|Autism Lesson Ask]]>><</link>>
<br>
<<icon "antiques/antique-watch.gif">><<link [[Push through and pretend you followed it|Autism Lesson Push]]>><</link>>
<br>
<<icon "antiques/antique-watch.gif">><<link [[Get out of the room (0:05)|Autism Lesson Leave]]>><<pass 5>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Lesson Ask
<<set $outside to 0>><<set $location to "school">><<schooleffects>><<effects>>
<<set _teacherKnows to autismTeacherKnows($autismLessonTeacher)>>
<<set _teacher to $autismLessonTeacher>>
<<set _teacherLove to C.npc[_teacher] ? C.npc[_teacher].love : 0>>
<<set _inTrouble to $delinquency gte setup.schoolDiscipline.slipThreshold>>
"Could you say it one thing at a time?" you ask. You concentrate on getting the sentence out clearly, and your voice comes out flatter than you intended.
<br><br>
<<if _teacherKnows>>
	Your teacher glances towards the note with your adjustments, then changes how <<he>> gives the instruction.
	<br><br>
	<<switch _teacher>>
		<<case "River">>
			"Alright. First, read the question. Don't worry about the working yet." River writes that on the board, then adds the second step underneath it. "When the first part's done, move to this one."
		<<case "Sirris">>
			"Of course. I should have separated it before you had to ask," Sirris says. <<He>> writes the first instruction on the board, waits until you find it, then writes the second underneath.
		<<case "Doren">>
			Doren looks back at what <<he>> wrote. "Yeah, I ran the steps together again. Sorry." <<He>> rewrites them as two separate lines. "Do the first one, then come back to the second."
		<<default>>
			Winter checks the adjustment note and gives a small nod. "Very well. First complete the question. After that, show your working. I will leave both instructions on the board."
	<</switch>>
	<br><br>
	The order is now clear, and you can catch up without trying to hold both instructions in your head at once.
	<<stress -6>><<lstress>><<run autismRestBreak(4)>>
<<else>>
	<<switch _teacher>>
		<<case "River">>
			River does not ask why you need it. "First, read the question. When that's done, show your working."
			<br><br>
			<<He>> moves on to somebody else's raised hand, leaving you with two clear steps.
			<<stress -4>><<lstress>><<run autismRestBreak(3)>>
		<<case "Sirris">>
			"Of course. We can slow it down," Sirris says, far too warmly, and comes over to your desk. <<He>> gives you the first instruction, waits while you follow it, then gives the second.
			<br><br>
			A few people look round. You would rather <<he>> had simply repeated it from the front, but you do have the instruction now.
			<<stress -2>><<llstress>><<run autismRestBreak(2)>>
		<<case "Doren">>
			Doren stops speaking and looks back at the board. "Yeah, I gave you too much at once there. Sorry."
			<br><br>
			<<He>> starts again with only the first instruction, waits until you have it, then gives the second. Half the class has stopped listening by then, which <<he>> does not seem to mind.
			<<stress -4>><<lstress>><<run autismRestBreak(3)>>
		<<default>>
			<<if _teacherLove gte 30 and !_inTrouble>>
				Winter looks annoyed, but <<he>> repeats the instruction in two parts and pauses between them.
				<br><br>
				"In future, ask as soon as you realise you have lost the instruction. Waiting until I have repeated myself twice wastes your time and mine."
				You have the instruction, but <span class="teal">the exchange was uncomfortable</span>.<<stress -2>><<llstress>><<run autismRestBreak(2)>>
			<<else>>
				"I have already repeated the instruction twice," Winter says. "I am not stopping the rest of the class to repeat it a third time."
				<br><br>
				<<He>> returns to the front. Several students look at you before turning back to their work, while you still do not know what instruction you missed.
				<<stress 6>><<gstress>><<run autismSocialDemand(3)>>
				<<delinquency 2.5>><<ggdelinquency>>
			<</if>>
	<</switch>>
<</if>>
<br><br>
<<set _autismNext to ($autismLessonReturn || "Hallways")>><<link "Next" _autismNext>><<unset $autismLessonReturn, $autismLessonTeacher, $autismLessonScene>><<endevent>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Lesson Push
<<set $outside to 0>><<set $location to "school">><<schooleffects>><<effects>>
<<person1>>
"I'm with you," you say, even though you are not sure which instruction the class is following. You pick up your pen so your teacher will move on.
<br><br>
You copy what is written on the board and watch the people around you to work out which part they are doing. At the same time, you keep checking your expression so the teacher will believe you understood the question. The pretending takes attention away from the work, leaving you farther behind even though your pen is moving.
<<stress 5>><<gstress>>
<<run autismSocialDemand(3)>>
<br><br>
<<set _autismNext to ($autismLessonReturn || "Hallways")>><<link "Next" _autismNext>><<unset $autismLessonReturn, $autismLessonTeacher, $autismLessonScene>><<endevent>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Lesson Leave
<<set $outside to 0>><<set $location to "school">><<schooleffects>><<effects>>
<<set _teacherKnows to autismTeacherKnows($autismLessonTeacher)>>
<<set _teacher to $autismLessonTeacher>>
<<set _teacherLove to C.npc[_teacher] ? C.npc[_teacher].love : 0>>
<<person1>>
You stand while the lesson is still going and walk straight to the door. Several students watch you leave, but you keep moving until you are in the corridor.
<br><br>
The corridor lights are brighter than the classroom, but the room full of voices is behind you now. With the noise gone,
you can think more clearly.
<<run autismRestBreak(6)>><<stress -4>><<lstress>>
<br><br>
<<if _teacherKnows>>
	Nobody comes after you. When you look in later, your work is on your desk where you left it, with the next page marked,
	and nothing has been written down anywhere.
<<else>>
	<<switch _teacher>>
		<<case "Doren">>
			Doren catches you in the corridor afterwards, looking apologetic about <<his>> own errand. "I do have to put it down,"
			<<he>> says. "Walking out. I'm sorry, it isn't up to me." <<He>> hesitates. "Was it the noise? You can tell me if it was."
			<<delinquency 5>><<ggdelinquency>>
		<<case "River">>
			River does not follow you into the corridor. By the end of the day, <<he>> has recorded that you left the lesson without permission.
			<<delinquency 7.5>><<ggdelinquency>>
		<<case "Sirris">>
			Sirris finds you before the next bell, all concern, and asks three questions you do not want to answer before
			mentioning that <<he>> has had to record it.
			<<delinquency 7.5>><<ggdelinquency>>
		<<default>>
			Winter does not follow you. <<He>> records the incident immediately and later mentions it to Leighton as well.
			<<delinquency 11>><<gggdelinquency>>
	<</switch>>
	<<if _teacherLove gte 40>>
		<br><br>
		Whatever else <<he>> has written, <<he>> has not written that you were being difficult.
	<</if>>
<</if>>
<br><br>
<<set _autismNext to ($autismLessonReturn || "Hallways")>><<link "Next" _autismNext>><<unset $autismLessonReturn, $autismLessonTeacher, $autismLessonScene>><<endevent>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Disclose Jordan
<<effects>><<npc Jordan>><<person1>>
<<if autismHasDocumentation()>>
	<<run autismDiscloseTo("Jordan")>>
	You hand Jordan the summary of your assessment. <<He>> reads it carefully, then returns it to you.
	<br><br>
	"Thank you for telling me, my child. Does this describe what has been troubling you here?"
	<br><br>
	You explain how the noise and activity in the quarters can wear you down, and how much effort it takes to keep your movements and expression under control.
	<br><br>
	"You may sit behind the screen by your bunk when you need a quiet break," <<he>> says. "There is no need to keep your hands still for my sake. If an instruction is unclear, ask me to explain it. I would rather repeat myself than leave you struggling with a task you do not understand."
	<br><br>
	"Your duties still need doing. If you need a break during them, tell me where you are going, and we can agree when you will return. I will not pass this report around the quarters."
<<else>>
	You check your bag, but have no assessment report to show Jordan.
<</if>>
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Temple Jordan]]>><<endevent>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism Unmask
<<set _autismState to ensureAutismSpectrumState()>>
<<set _back to _autismState.unmaskReturn || "Bedroom">>
<<effects>>
<<set _loadBefore to ensureAutismSpectrumState().load>>
<<run autismUnmask(15)>>
<<set _loadDrop to _loadBefore - ensureAutismSpectrumState().load>>
<<if _back is "Temple Bunk">>
	You sit behind the dressing screen beside your bunk, out of sight of the other initiates.
	<br><br>
<<elseif _back is "Eden Cabin">>
	You settle into a corner of the cabin and give yourself a few minutes without trying to look busy.
	<br><br>
<</if>>
You stop checking whether your expression looks interested. You let your eyes rest where they naturally want to look, and you stop forcing your hands to stay still.
<br><br>
<<if _loadDrop gte 4>>
	After a few minutes, you notice that you have more attention left for your own thoughts when you are not checking your face and hands. You remain that way for the rest of the break.
	<<tiredness -3>><<lltiredness>>
<<elseif _loadDrop gt 0>>
	You catch yourself correcting your expression a few times out of habit. Each time, you stop and let your face relax again.
	<<tiredness -2>><<ltiredness>>
<<elseif autismDeliberateSpent()>>
	You are already too tired for the change to make much difference tonight, but you stop checking yourself anyway.
	You sit quietly without rehearsing how you should look or respond.
	<<tiredness -2>><<ltiredness>>
<<else>>
	You were not masking very heavily, so the change is small. You still let your face and hands move naturally for a while.
	<<tiredness -1>><<ltiredness>>
<</if>>
<br><br>
<<link "Next" _back>><<run delete $autismSpectrum.unmaskReturn>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism High Street Overload
<<set $weekly.autismTownAwareness to true>>
<<run autismSocialDemand(2)>>
<<generate1>><<person1>>
The street is crowded, with people moving past you in both directions. Shop signs keep changing colour,
the crossing signal sounds over the traffic, and several people are talking nearby at the same time.
<br><br>
A promoter carrying a clipboard steps in front of you. "Got a minute?"
<<He>> starts talking about whatever <<hes>> promoting before you can answer.
<br><br>
Before you can work out what <<he>> wants from you, someone bumps your shoulder as they pass.
Another voice calls from behind <<him>>, while <<he>> keeps talking without stopping.
<br><br>
You can hear what <<hes>> saying, but there is too much happening
at once for you to work out what <<he>> wants you to answer.
<br><br>
<<link [[Ask <<him>> to state the question plainly|Autism High Street Overload Direct]]>><</link>>
<br>
<<link [[Step into a quiet doorway (0:10)|Autism High Street Overload Quiet]]>><<pass 10>><</link>>
<br>
<<link [[Nod and let <<him>> continue (0:05)|Autism High Street Overload Mask]]>><<pass 5>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism High Street Overload Direct
<<location "high">><<effects>>
<<run autismRestBreak(2)>>
"What do you need me to answer?" you ask.
<br><br>
The promoter checks <<his>> clipboard. "Will you sign up to make a weekly donation?"
<br><br>
"No," you say.
<br><br>
<<He>> nods and steps aside, letting you continue down the street. 
Once <<he>> asks you directly,
the conversation is <span class="green">much easier to deal with</span>.<<stress -2>><<lstress>>
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|High Street]]>><<endevent>><<set $eventskip to 1>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism High Street Overload Quiet
<<location "high">><<effects>>
<<run autismRestBreak(3)>>
You leave the promoter talking and move into a quiet doorway beside one of the shops.
<br><br>
The doorway keeps you away from most people walking past and reduces some of the street noise. You close your eyes and remain there until your breathing slows and you can separate the nearby sounds again.
When you return to the pavement,
it is <span class="green">easier to focus</span> on what is directly in front of you.<<stress -3>><<lstress>>
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|High Street]]>><<endevent>><<set $eventskip to 1>><</link>>
<br>

:: Autism High Street Overload Mask
<<location "high">><<effects>>
<<run autismSocialDemand(2)>>
You keep nodding whenever the promoter pauses and smile when <<he>> smiles.
<br><br>
You stopped following what <<he>> was saying a while ago,
but <<he>> assumes you understand and continues towards the shopping centre.
<br><br>
Rather than ask <<him>> to repeat everything, you keep following and try to respond at the right times.
<br><br>
When <<he>> finally thanks you and leaves, you are standing inside the shopping centre.
Pretending you understood the whole conversation has left you <span class="red">more stressed</span>
than simply asking <<him>> to explain it again.<<stress 7>><<ggstress>>
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Shopping Centre]]>><<endevent>><<set $eventskip to 1>><</link>>
<br>

:: Kylar Autism Masking
<<effects>><<set $daily.kylarAutismMask to true>>
Kylar stops drawing and looks at your hands. You have been holding them together in your lap to stop yourself fidgeting, while keeping the same calm expression you use around other people.
<br><br>
"You're doing it again," <<he>> says. "Trying to look fine."
<br><br>
You tell <<him>> that you are fine. You have already said the same thing several times today,
and nobody else questioned it.
<br><br>
"I know," Kylar says. <<He>> closes the sketchbook around <<his>> pencil, keeping <<his>> place.
"I do the same thing. Watching your hands, keeping your face right, thinking about how you're sitting.
You were already doing it when I got here."
<br><br>
Your fingers press harder against each other in your lap.
<br><br>
Kylar does not ask what is wrong. <<He>> turns partly away so you are no longer being watched directly, but stays beside you.
<br><br>
<<link [[Stop pretending while <<he>> looks away (0:20)|Kylar Autism Masking Rest]]>><<pass 20>><<npcincr Kylar love 1>><<npcincr Kylar rage -1>><</link>><<glove>><<lksuspicion>>
<br>
<<link [[Keep pretending to be fine|Kylar Autism Masking Hold]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Kylar Autism Masking Rest
<<effects>>
<<run autismUnmask(20)>><<run autismRestBreak(8)>>
You let your shoulders drop and stop forcing your hands to stay still. Your fingers begin moving the way they normally do when you are alone. Kylar keeps <<his>> body turned partly away so <<hes>> not watching you.
<br><br>
For the next twenty minutes, <<he>> keeps drawing with <<his>> back partly turned.
<<He>> does not ask you to talk or pay attention to <<him>>, so you can sit quietly and move however feels comfortable.
<br><br>
When you eventually speak, Kylar answers normally and continues without commenting on the long silence. <span class="green">You feel less tired</span> after spending some time without checking every movement.<<stress -8>><<llstress>>
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Kylar Autism Return]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Kylar Autism Masking Hold
<<effects>>
<<run autismSocialDemand(4)>>
You keep your hands still and tell Kylar again that you are fine. <<He>> watches you for a few seconds,
clearly unconvinced, but does not push you to explain.
<br><br>
Instead, <<he>> opens the sketchbook and returns to the same page. <<He>> does not mention it again, but you keep spending effort on holding your hands still and controlling your expression even though <<he>> already noticed.
<br><br> Pretending you are fine <span class="red">takes even more effort</span> when Kylar already knows
something is wrong.<<stress 4>><<gstress>>
<br><br>
<<link [[Next|Kylar Autism Return]]>><</link>>
<br>

:: Widgets Autism [widget]
<<widget "autism-unmask">>
	<<if autismCanUnmask(_args[0] || $passage)>>
		<<set _unmaskBack to _args[0] || $passage>>
		<<icon "antiques/antique-watch.gif">><<link [[Drop the mask (0:15)|Autism Unmask]]>><<pass 15>><<run $autismSpectrum.unmaskReturn = _unmaskBack>><</link>>
		<br>
	<</if>>
<</widget>>

<<widget "autism-burnout-line">>
	<<if playerHasAutismSpectrum() and autismSpectrumPhase() is "burnout" and !$statFreeze>>
		<<set _abLine to autismBurnoutLine(_args[0] || "social")>>
		<<if _abLine>>
			<span class="purple"><<print _abLine>></span>
			<br><br>
		<</if>>
	<</if>>
<</widget>>

<<widget "autism-focus-line">>
	<<if playerHasAutismSpectrum() and _args[0] and autismIsFocus(_args[0]) and !$daily.autismFocusLine and !$statFreeze>>
		<<set $daily.autismFocusLine to 1>>
		<span class="gold"><<print autismFocusEngagedLine()>></span>
		<br><br>
	<</if>>
<</widget>>

<<widget "autism-npc">>
	<<set _an to _args[0]>>
	<<if playerHasAutismSpectrum() and _an and C.npc[_an] and C.npc[_an].init is 1 and !$daily["autismNpc_" + _an]>>
		<<set _aPhase to autismSpectrumPhase()>>
		<<set _aMaskHigh to autismMaskingHigh()>>
		<<set _aAgo to autismOverloadDaysAgo()>>
		<<set _aBond to Math.max(C.npc[_an].love, C.npc[_an].trust)>>
		<<set _aClose to _aBond gte 65>>
		<<set _aKnown to _aBond gte 35>>
		<<switch _an>>
			<<case "Whitney" "Sydney">><<set _aHasLine to true>>
			<<case "Eden">><<set _aHasLine to _aPhase is "burnout" or _aAgo gte 0 or _aMaskHigh>>
			<<case "Avery">><<set _aHasLine to _aMaskHigh or _aPhase is "burnout">>
			<<case "Alex">><<set _aHasLine to _aPhase is "burnout" or (_aClose and _aMaskHigh)>>
			<<case "Leighton">><<set _aHasLine to autismSchoolPlan() and _aPhase is "burnout">>
			<<default>><<set _aHasLine to false>>
		<</switch>>
		<<if _aHasLine and (_aAgo gte 0 or _aPhase isnot "settled" or _aMaskHigh)>>
			<<set $daily["autismNpc_" + _an] to 1>>
			<<switch _an>>
				<<case "Whitney">>
					<<if _aClose>>
						<<if _aPhase is "burnout">>
							Whitney takes one look at you and clicks <<nnpc_his "Whitney">> tongue. "Damn, bitch, you look fucking exhausted."
							<br><br>
							<<nnpc_He "Whitney">> jerks <<nnpc_his "Whitney">> head towards the others. "Fuck off without us. I'll catch up."
							<br><br>
							They leave. You start to say you can manage on your own, but Whitney cuts you off. "Don't start. I'm not babysitting you. You walk slow, I walk slow. That's all."
							<br><br>
							<<nnpc_He "Whitney">> stays beside you through the next two corridors, matching your pace without asking you to explain anything.
							<<stress -5>><<lstress>>
						<<elseif _aMaskHigh>>
							Whitney squints at you while you answer someone. "Why're you talking like that, slut? That fake polite voice you do when you're trying too hard."
							<br><br>
							<<nnpc_He "Whitney">> gives an exaggerated imitation of your careful tone, then snorts. "Pack it in. It's just me. I already know you're weird."
							<br><br>
							<<nnpc_He "Whitney">> keeps walking beside you as if the matter is settled.
							<<stress -3>><<lstress>>
						<<else>>
							"You've gone quiet," Whitney says after trying to get a reaction from you twice. "Proper quiet. Not your sulking bullshit."
							<br><br>
							When you still do not answer, <<nnpc_he "Whitney">> starts talking about people from school, who is sleeping with whom, and which teacher pissed <<nnpc_him "Whitney">> off that morning. <<nnpc_He "Whitney">> keeps talking without stopping to demand a reply.
							<br><br>
							By the time you reach the stairs, you realise <<nnpc_he "Whitney">> has been carrying the conversation so you do not have to.
							<<stress -3>><<lstress>>
						<</if>>
					<<elseif _aKnown>>
						<<if _aAgo gte 0>>
							Whitney catches you looking over and lowers <<nnpc_his "Whitney">> voice. "That weird shit you did the other day. You do that often, or was school just being extra fucking awful?"
							<br><br>
							You hesitate. <<nnpc_He "Whitney">> rolls <<nnpc_his "Whitney">> eyes before you can force an answer out. "Forget it. I wasn't asking you to explain everything."
						<<else>>
							Whitney notices your hands moving, then looks at your face. <<nnpc_He "Whitney">> opens <<nnpc_his "Whitney">> mouth as if to make a joke, changes <<nnpc_his "Whitney">> mind, and carries on walking.
						<</if>>
					<<else>>
						<<if _aMaskHigh>>
							"What the fuck's wrong with you, bitch?" Whitney says. "Every time someone talks to you, you change your face before you answer like you're scared you'll make the wrong expression."
							<br><br>
							You immediately become aware of how still you are sitting. Whitney grins when <<nnpc_he "Whitney">> sees you react.
							<<stress 5>><<gstress>><<run autismSocialDemand(2)>>
						<<else>>
							Whitney says something to you, but you are still working out what <<nnpc_he "Whitney">> said when <<nnpc_he "Whitney">> repeats it louder. "Oi, slut. I said, are you fucking listening?"
							<br><br>
							Several people nearby turn towards you as Whitney waits for an answer.
							<<stress 4>><<gstress>><<run autismSocialDemand(2)>>
						<</if>>
					<</if>>

				<<case "Sydney">>
					<<if _aClose>>
						<<if _aPhase is "burnout" or _aMaskHigh>>
							Sydney stops halfway through a story and watches you struggle to keep reacting. "Sorry. I've been filling every bit of silence because I thought I was helping, haven't I?"
							<br><br>
							<<nnpc_He "Sydney">> folds <<nnpc_his "Sydney">> hands in <<nnpc_his "Sydney">> lap. "You don't have to answer me. I'll wait until you actually want to say something."
							<br><br>
							Sydney stays quiet until you decide to speak.
							<<stress -4>><<lstress>>
						<<else>>
							"Sometimes I can't tell whether you're still thinking or whether I've upset you," Sydney says. "I don't mind waiting. I only wanted to make sure I wasn't hurting you without noticing."
							<br><br>
							You tell <<nnpc_him "Sydney">> that <<nnpc_he "Sydney">> did nothing wrong. <<nnpc_He "Sydney">> smiles with visible relief and lets the subject end there.
							<<stress -2>><<llstress>>
						<</if>>
					<<elseif _aKnown>>
						Sydney notices you looking towards the quieter chair and moves <<nnpc_his "Sydney">> things from it before you have to ask. "Here. This one's farther from everyone."
					<<else>>
						Sydney asks you a question and waits. Before you finish deciding how to answer, <<nnpc_he "Sydney">> starts worrying that the question upset you.
						<br><br>
						"Sorry, was that too personal? I can ask it another way." <<nnpc_He "Sydney">> starts again while you are still working on the first version.
					<</if>>

				<<case "Eden">>
					<<if _aPhase is "burnout" or _aAgo gte 0>>
						Eden studies you for a few seconds, then points to the chair nearest <<nnpc_him "Eden">>.
						<br><br>
						"Sit here. I don't want you wandering off while you're like this." <<nnpc_He "Eden">> handles the firewood, water, and food without asking you to help, only speaking when <<nnpc_he "Eden">> needs you to move or hand something over.
						<br><br>
						<<if _aClose>>
							Whenever you try to get up and help, Eden tells you to sit back down. You spend most of the afternoon quietly within <<nnpc_his "Eden">> reach while <<nnpc_he "Eden">> works around you.
							<<stress -8>><<lstress>>
						<<else>>
							Eden decides where you sit, what you do, and when you eat without asking. Most of those choices are things you would have picked yourself, but that does not make being ordered around less irritating.
							<<stress -4>><<lstress>>
						<</if>>
					<<elseif _aMaskHigh>>
						Eden watches you force another polite answer out. "Stop doing that. You pretend for everyone outside all day. I know what you're like."
					<</if>>

				<<case "Avery">>
					<<if _aMaskHigh>>
						Avery waits until you finish arranging your expression before <<nnpc_he "Avery">> answers. "You are spending a remarkable amount of effort deciding how to look during an ordinary conversation."
						<br><br>
						<<nnpc_He "Avery">> glances at your hands. "You are doing the same thing there. If this is for my benefit, you may stop."
						<br><br>
						<<nnpc_He "Avery">> changes the subject before you decide whether the comment was permission or criticism.
						<<stress 4>><<gstress>>
					<<elseif _aPhase is "burnout">>
						"You are responding much more slowly than usual this evening," Avery says after waiting through another pause. "I have no interest in dragging a ten-minute conversation across an hour."
						<br><br>
						<<nnpc_He "Avery">> ends the meeting early. Whether <<nnpc_he "Avery">> did it for you or for <<nnpc_him "Avery">> remains unclear.
					<</if>>

				<<case "Alex">>
					<<if _aPhase is "burnout">>
						Alex watches you struggle through the first job, looks at the list, then scratches two jobs out.
						<br><br>
						"Finish feeding them, then get yourself inside," <<nnpc_he "Alex">> says. "Fence'll still be broken tomorrow. Animals won't care if you take one afternoon off."
						<br><br>
						<<nnpc_He "Alex">> goes back to work before you can argue, leaving you with only the feed to finish.
						<<stress -5>><<lstress>><<run autismRestBreak(4)>>
					<<elseif _aClose and _aMaskHigh>>
						You have worked beside Alex for nearly an hour without saying much. <<nnpc_He "Alex">> finally glances over. "You know you don't have to make conversation out here, right? Cows aren't going to think you're rude."
						<br><br>
						<<nnpc_He "Alex">> gives a small smile and gets back to work.
						<<stress -3>><<lstress>>
					<</if>>

				<<case "Leighton">>
					<<if autismSchoolPlan()>>
						<<if _aPhase is "burnout">>
							Leighton passes you in the corridor, gets several steps farther, then stops.
							<br><br>
							"You look as though the next lesson is going to end with somebody carrying you to the infirmary," <<nnpc_he "Leighton">> says. <<nnpc_He "Leighton">> points down the corridor. "Save us both the paperwork and walk there yourself."
							<br><br>
							<<nnpc_He "Leighton">> continues on without waiting to see whether you obey.
						<</if>>
					<</if>>

				<</switch>>
			<br><br>
		<</if>>
	<</if>>
<</widget>>

<<widget "autism-school-day">>
	<<if playerHasAutismSpectrum() and Time.schoolDay and Time.hour gte 16 and !$daily.autismSchoolDay and autismSchoolDayCount() gt 0>>
		<<set $daily.autismSchoolDay to 1>>
		<<include "Autism School Day">>
		<<exitAll>>
	<</if>>
<</widget>>

<<widget "autism-service">>
	<<if playerHasAutismSpectrum() and autismSpectrumPhase() isnot "settled" and !autismInMeltdown() and !$weekly.autismService>>
		<<set $weekly.autismService to 1>>
		<<set $autismServiceReturn to _args[0] || $passage>>
		<<include "Autism Service Stall">>
		<<set _autismServiceFired to true>>
	<</if>>
<</widget>>

<<widget "autism-suspension">>
	<<if playerHasAutismSpectrum() and schoolSuspended() and !$daily.autismSuspension>>
		<<set $daily.autismSuspension to 1>>
		<<set $autismSuspensionReturn to _args[0] || $passage>>
		<<include "Autism Suspension Day">>
		<<exitAll>>
	<</if>>
<</widget>>

<<widget "autism-cold-start">>
	<<if playerHasAutismSpectrum() and autismSpectrumPhase() isnot "settled" and !autismInMeltdown() and $exposed lte 0>>
		<<set _coldKind to _args[0] || "task">>
		<<if !$weekly["autismCold_" + _coldKind]>>
			<<set _coldBack to _args[1] || $passage>>
			<<set _coldCost to _args[2]>>
			<<set $weekly["autismCold_" + _coldKind] to 1>>
			<<set $autismColdKind to _coldKind>>
			<<run $autismSpectrum.coldReturn = _coldBack>>
			<<if _coldCost>><<run $autismSpectrum.coldCost = _coldCost>><<else>><<run delete $autismSpectrum.coldCost>><</if>>
			<<include "Autism Cold Start">>
			<<set _autismColdFired to true>>
		<</if>>
	<</if>>
<</widget>>

<<widget "autism-cold-cost">>
	<<if $autismSpectrum and $autismSpectrum.coldCost>>
		<br>
		<<if _args[0]>>
			You spent your <<= $autismSpectrum.coldCost>> just getting started. <span class="blue">You still have the task itself to do</span>.
		<<else>>
			You spent your <<= $autismSpectrum.coldCost>> trying to start, but <span class="blue">the task is still waiting</span>.
		<</if>>
	<</if>>
<</widget>>

<<widget "autism-disclose-jordan">>
	<<if autismHasDocumentation() and !autismDisclosedTo("Jordan")>>
		<<socialiseicon "ask">><<link [[Show Jordan your documentation (0:10)|Autism Disclose Jordan]]>><<pass 10>><</link>>
		<br>
	<</if>>
<</widget>>

<<widget "autism-disclose-bailey">>
	<<if autismHasDocumentation() and !autismDisclosedTo("Bailey")>>
		<<socialiseicon "ask">><<link [[Show Bailey your documentation (0:10)|Autism Disclose Bailey]]>><<pass 10>><</link>>
		<br>
	<</if>>
<</widget>>

<<widget "autism-kylar-quiet">>
	<<if playerHasAutismSpectrum() and $bus is "kylarmanor" and $autismSpectrum.kylarDiscussed and C.npc.Kylar.love gte 70 and !$autismSpectrum.kylarQuietSeen>>
		<<set _kylarQuietBack to $passage>>
		<<socialiseicon>><<link [[Spend the afternoon in Kylar's room (1:00)|Autism Kylar Quiet]]>><<pass 60>><<set $autismSpectrum.kylarQuietSeen to true>><<run $autismSpectrum.kylarQuietReturn = _kylarQuietBack>><</link>>
		<br>
	<</if>>
<</widget>>

<<widget "autism-kylar-recognition">>
	<<if playerHasAutismSpectrum() and $autismSpectrum.kylarDiscussed and !$daily.kylarAutismRecognition and C.npc.Kylar.love gte 40>>
		<<set $daily.kylarAutismRecognition to 1>>
		<<set _kylarClose to C.npc.Kylar.love gte 70>>
		<<set _recognitionRoll to random(1, 4)>>
		<<if _recognitionRoll is 1>>
			"You do the thing with the sentences," Kylar says suddenly. "Where you say the same one you said
			last time. I noticed because I've got ones I use as well."
			<br><br>
			<<if _kylarClose>>
				<<He>> counts them off. "'That's really interesting.' 'I hadn't thought of it like that.'
				'Sorry, say again?'" A pause. "I've got about nine. They're for when I can't make a new one fast enough."
				<<He>> lowers <<his>> voice at the end and looks down at the page.
				<<stress -3>><<lstress>>
			<<else>>
				<<He>> does not elaborate, and looks like <<he>> wishes <<he>> had not started.
			<</if>>
		<<elseif _recognitionRoll is 2>>
			"Can I ask something," Kylar says. "When you said about the lights. In the corridor. Does that mean the
			buzzing, or does everyone hear the buzzing?"
			<br><br>
			<<if _kylarClose>>
				You tell <<him>> that not everyone hears it. Kylar stops speaking and looks down at the page.
				<br><br>
				"I've been complaining about that since I was nine," <<he>> says. "Everyone said I was
				being dramatic." <<He>> gives a short laugh, then stops and looks down.
			<<else>>
				You say you are not sure. <<He>> nods slowly and does not bring it up again today.
			<</if>>
		<<elseif _recognitionRoll is 3>>
			Partway through the afternoon, you realise you have stopped checking your expression and planning every reply.
			<br><br>
			You have let several silences pass without forcing yourself to speak. One of them lasted more than a minute.
			<br><br>
			<<if _kylarClose>>
				Kylar has not tried to fill the silences either. <<He>> keeps reading, sometimes saying
				something about the page without waiting for you to answer.
				<<stress -4>><<lstress>>
			<<else>>
				Kylar does not seem to have noticed the silences at all.
				<<stress -2>><<llstress>>
			<</if>>
		<<else>>
			"I looked some of it up," Kylar admits. "After you showed me. Not about you," <<he>> adds quickly.
			"Just... generally."
			<br><br>
			<<if _kylarClose>>
				<<He>> turns <<his>> pen over twice. "Some of it was quite hard to read."
				<br><br>
				<<He>> does not say why. You do not ask, and <<his>> shoulders come down
				a little when you don't.
			<<else>>
				"Anyway. It was interesting." Kylar pauses, then starts talking about something else.
			<</if>>
		<</if>>
		<br><br>
	<</if>>
<</widget>>

<<widget "autism-disclose-school">>
	<<if autismHasDocumentation() and $exposed lte 0 and Time.schoolDay and $schoolstate isnot "early" and $schoolstate isnot "late">>
		<<if autismPrefersPrivate() and !autismSchoolPlan() and !$autismSpectrum.privateNudged>>
			<<set $autismSpectrum.privateNudged to true>>
			<<ind>>You have the report in your bag. You told Harper you would rather nobody
			knew, and <span class="blue">you have not changed your mind about that</span>.
			<br>
		<</if>>
		<<if !autismSchoolPlan()>>
			<<schoolicon "headdesk">><<link [[Take your documentation to the office (0:15)|Autism Disclose Leighton]]>><<pass 15>><</link>>
			<br>
		<</if>>
		<<if Time.schoolTerm>>
			<<set _undisclosed to ["River", "Sirris", "Doren", "Winter"].filter(t => !autismDisclosedTo(t))>>
			<<if _undisclosed.length>>
				<<set $autismDiscloseTarget to _undisclosed.random()>>
				<<socialiseicon "ask">><<link [[Show your documentation to a teacher (0:10)|Autism Disclose Teacher]]>><<pass 10>><</link>>
				<br>
			<</if>>
		<</if>>
	<</if>>
<</widget>>

<<widget "autism-disclose-nurse">>
	<<if autismHasDocumentation() and !autismDisclosedTo("nurse")>>
		<<schoolicon "infirmary">><<link [[Show the nurse your documentation (0:10)|Autism Disclose Nurse]]>><<pass 10>><</link>>
		<br>
	<</if>>
<</widget>>

<<widget "autism-compose">>
	<<if playerHasAutismSpectrum() and !autismComposed()>>
		<<set _composeBack to _args[0] || $passage>>
		<<icon "antiques/antique-watch.gif">><<link [[Rehearse for the next conversation (0:15)|Autism Compose]]>><<pass 15>><<run $autismSpectrum.composeReturn = _composeBack>><</link>>
		<br>
	<</if>>
<</widget>>

<<widget "autism-assessment">>
	<<if !$possessed and $combat isnot 1 and autismAssessmentStage() is "intake">>
		<<if !autismAssessmentHasSource("self") and ["Bedroom", "Bathroom", "Farm Bedroom", "Flats Poppy Bedroom", "School Library", "Temple Bunk", "Eden Cabin", "Wolf Cave"].includes($passage)>>
			<<set _assessBack to $passage>>
			<<icon "antiques/antique-watch.gif">><<link [[Fill in the assessment questionnaires (1:00)|Autism Assessment Questionnaire]]>><<pass 60>><<run $autismSpectrum.assessmentReturn = _assessBack>><</link>>
			<br>
		<</if>>
	<</if>>
<</widget>>

<<widget "autism-assessment-school">>
	<<if autismAssessmentStage() is "intake" and $exposed lte 0>>
		<<if !autismAssessmentHasSource("school")>>
			<<schoolicon "headdesk">><<link [[Take the assessment form to the office (0:10)|Autism Assessment Records]]>><<pass 10>><</link>>
			<br>
		<</if>>
		<<if !autismAssessmentHasSource("teacher") and Time.schoolTerm>>
			<<set $autismAssessmentTeacher to ["River", "Sirris", "Doren", "Winter"].random()>>
			<<socialiseicon "ask">><<link [[Ask a teacher to fill in the assessment form (0:10)|Autism Assessment Teacher]]>><<pass 10>><</link>>
			<br>
		<</if>>
	<</if>>
<</widget>>

<<widget "autism-assessment-guardian">>
	<<if autismAssessmentStage() is "intake" and !autismAssessmentHasSource("guardian") and !$autismSpectrum.guardianRefused>>
		<<socialiseicon "ask">><<link [[Ask Bailey to fill in the assessment form (0:10)|Autism Assessment Guardian]]>><<pass 10>><</link>>
		<br>
	<</if>>
<</widget>>

<<widget "autism-focus">>
	<<if playerHasAutismSpectrum() and !$possessed and $combat isnot 1 and !$daily.autismFocusDwell>>
		<<set _focusBack to _args[0] || $passage>>
		<<icon "antiques/antique-watch.gif">><<link [[Spend some time on <<= autismHyperfixationName()>> (0:30)|Autism Focus]]>><<pass 30>><<set $daily.autismFocusDwell to 1>><<run $autismSpectrum.focusReturn = _focusBack>><</link>>
		<br>
	<</if>>
<</widget>>

<<widget "autism-bailey">>
	<<if playerHasAutismSpectrum() and $renttime lt 0 and autismSpectrumPhase() isnot "settled">>
		<br>
		<<if autismRentCanPay()>>
			You have had the money for days and knew when the rent was due. Each morning, you reminded yourself to pay it, but you kept putting off the part where you had to go to Bailey and hand over the money.
		<<else>>
			You remembered the rent every day this week. Each reminder made you think about what you still needed to do, but you never started dealing with it.
		<</if>>
		<br>
		<span id="autismBaileyExplain">
			<<link "Try to explain why it's late">>
				<<replace "#autismBaileyExplain">>
					<<include "Autism Rent Explain">>
				<</replace>>
			<</link>>
		</span>
		<br>
	<</if>>
<</widget>>

<<widget "autism-aftermath-kylar">>
	<<if playerHasAutismSpectrum() and autismOverloadDaysAgo() gte 0 and $autismSpectrum.kylarDiscussed and !$daily.kylarAutismAftermath>>
		<<set $daily.kylarAutismAftermath to 1>>
		<<set _form to autismLastOverloadForm()>>
		<<if _form is "shutdown">>
			Kylar has clearly been waiting to say something. <<He>> starts before you have properly settled down.
			<br><br>
			"You weren't answering me the other day. I said your name twice, and then I thought maybe you couldn't hear me, so I said it louder, which was probably stupid." <<He>> grips <<his>> sleeve. "I looked it up afterwards. I didn't know people could understand what was happening and still not be able to answer."
		<<else>>
			Kylar speaks before you have finished sitting down. "I saw what happened the other day. I nearly came over, then I thought another person talking at you would make it worse. Then I spent the rest of the day thinking I was a coward for leaving you alone."
		<</if>>
		<br><br>
		<<if C.npc.Kylar.love gte 50>>
			<<He>> leans forward. "Please tell me what you want me to do if it happens again. You can give me rules. Exact rules. I can follow those."
			<<stress -4>><<lstress>><<npcincr Kylar love 1>><<glove>>
		<<else>>
			Kylar starts another sentence, stops, and rubs <<his>> thumb over the edge of <<his>> sleeve instead. "Sorry. I know I'm making this about whether I did the right thing."
			<<stress -2>><<llstress>>
		<</if>>
		<br><br>
	<</if>>
<</widget>>

<<widget "autism-robin">>
	<<if playerHasAutismSpectrum() and !$daily.robin.autismNotice and C.npc.Robin.init is 1>>
		<<set _autismPhase to autismSpectrumPhase()>>
		<<set _autismAgo to autismOverloadDaysAgo()>>
		<<set _robinCares to Math.max(C.npc.Robin.love, C.npc.Robin.trust)>>
		/* Robin's own state limits what <<he>> has to offer, whatever <<his>> feelings. */
		<<set _robinSpare to C.npc.Robin.trauma lt 40>>
		<<if _autismAgo gte 0 or _autismPhase isnot "settled" or autismMaskingHigh() or _robinCares gte 30>>
			<<set $daily.robin.autismNotice to 1>>
			<<if _autismAgo gte 0>>
				<<if _robinCares gte 60 and _robinSpare>>
					Robin puts <<his>> controller down as soon as you sit. "I heard about <<if _autismAgo is 0>>what happened earlier<<else>>the other day<</if>>," <<he>> says, picking at the controller cable. "I wasn't going to ask you to explain it. I just... didn't want you thinking I'd heard and was being weird about you."
					<br><br>
					<<He>> turns the volume down and shifts over to leave you more room. "We can just play something. Or not play anything. Whatever's easier."
					<<stress -3>><<lstress>><<npcincr Robin love 1>><<glove>>
				<<elseif _robinCares gte 30>>
					"Someone said you had a really bad time," Robin says. <<He>> looks down almost immediately. "Sorry. That sounded stupid. I don't know what I'm supposed to say, I just wanted to check on you."
					<br><br>
					<<He>> moves over on the bed and leaves the rest of the space for you without asking another question.
					<<stress -1>><<llstress>>
				<<else>>
					Robin glances up from <<his>> controller. "Are you alright? You've seemed strange since the other day."
					<br><br>
					<<He>> hesitates. "I didn't do something, did I?"
				<</if>>
			<<elseif _autismPhase is "burnout">>
				<<if _robinCares gte 60 and _robinSpare>>
					Robin watches you struggle to answer a simple question, then stops asking more. "Do you want me to keep talking so you don't have to, or would you rather I shut up for a while?"
					<br><br>
					You only have to choose between the two, and Robin accepts the answer without asking you to explain it.
					<<stress -4>><<lstress>><<npcincr Robin love 1>><<glove>>
				<<elseif _robinCares gte 30>>
					"You look how I feel after a really bad week," Robin says. <<He>> winces at <<his>> own words. "I mean you can sit here without doing anything. I'm not saying you look awful."
					<<stress -2>><<llstress>>
				<<else>>
					Robin has to repeat <<himself>> before you react. <<He>> looks embarrassed and turns back to the screen. "Never mind. It wasn't important."
				<</if>>
			<<elseif autismMaskingHigh()>>
				<<if _robinCares gte 60 and _robinSpare>>
					Robin watches your hands for a while. "You know you don't have to keep doing the school version of you in here, right?"
					<br><br>
					You ask what <<he>> means. <<He>> gestures towards you. "Keeping your hands still. Making the right face before you answer. You do it all day, then you come in here and keep doing it with me."
					<br><br>
					<<if autismCanUnmask()>><span class="gold">You could stop hiding those movements here.</span><br><</if>>
				<<elseif _robinCares gte 30>>
					Robin notices you forcing your hands still, but <<he>> looks away before you can ask why <<he>> was staring.
				<</if>>
			<<elseif _autismPhase is "strained">>
				<<if _robinCares gte 60 and _robinSpare>>
					<<if autismSchoolDayHas("reckoning") and autismMaskingNoticed()>>
						"You're still acting like you're in class," Robin says without looking away from the screen. "You can stop checking every little thing you do. It's only me."
						<br><br>
						<<He>> turns the lamp down and starts a game without asking you to talk about school.
						<<stress -4>><<lstress>>
					<<elseif autismSchoolDayHas("quietroom")>>
						"Was the second half any better?" Robin asks. When you say it was easier after the quiet room, <<he>> smiles. "I'm glad something actually helped. You looked miserable this morning."
						<<stress -2>><<llstress>>
					<<else>>
						"You look exhausted," Robin says. <<He>> turns the lamp down. "You can tell me about it later if you want. You don't have to do it now."
						<<stress -2>><<llstress>>
					<</if>>
				<<elseif _robinCares gte 30>>
					Robin takes one look at you and keeps <<his>> questions to <<himself>>. "You can sit here if you want. I don't mind."
				<</if>>
			<<else>>
				<<if _robinCares gte 60>>
					"You've been talking about <<= autismHyperfixationName()>> a lot," Robin says. "I like it when you get onto that subject. You talk faster and stop checking whether I'm bored every five seconds."
					<br><br>
					<<He>> pulls <<his>> legs up to make room on the bed. "Tell me the part you keep skipping because you think I won't understand. I probably won't, but I can still listen."
					<<stress -3>><<lstress>><<npcincr Robin love 1>><<glove>>
				<<elseif _robinCares gte 30>>
					"You're still really into that <<= autismHyperfixationName()>> thing," Robin says. "You can tell me about it if you want. Just don't get mad if I lose track."
				<</if>>
			<</if>>
			<br><br>
		<</if>>
	<</if>>
<</widget>>

<<widget "autism-stim-result">>
	<<if _args[0] gte 2>>
		<span class="green">Your load drops.</span>
	<<elseif _args[0] gt 0>>
		<span class="teal">Your load drops a little.</span>
	<<else>>
		<span class="blue">Your load does not drop.</span>
		<<print [
			"You keep repeating the familiar movement for the full break instead of forcing yourself to stop.",
			"You still feel overloaded afterwards, but you gave yourself fifteen minutes without hiding the movement.",
			"The movement is familiar and easy to repeat, so you continue even though your load does not drop.",
			"You continue until the break ends. Your load stays the same, but you did not spend those minutes forcing yourself to stay still."
		].random()>>
	<</if>>
<</widget>>

<<widget "autism-stim">>
	<<if playerHasAutismSpectrum() and !$possessed and $combat isnot 1>>
		<<set _stimBack to _args[0] || $passage>>
		<<if autismStimIsMasked()>>
			<<icon "antiques/antique-watch.gif">><<link [[Stim, keeping it small (0:15)|Autism Stim]]>><<pass 15>><<run $autismSpectrum.stimReturn = _stimBack>><</link>>
		<<else>>
			<<icon "antiques/antique-watch.gif">><<link [[Stim (0:15)|Autism Stim]]>><<pass 15>><<run $autismSpectrum.stimReturn = _stimBack>><</link>>
		<</if>>
		<br>
	<</if>>
<</widget>>
